teps\" (i.e., number of distinct row lengths). The staircase shape has row lengths n-1, n-2, ..., 1, which are all distinct, so number of steps = n-1. So minimal rectangles = n-1. Indeed, for a Ferrers diagram (a Young diagram), the minimal number of rectangles needed to partition it is equal to the number of distinct row lengths. This is because each rectangle can cover a contiguous block of rows and columns, which correspond to a contiguous block of rows of same length? Something like that.\n\nLet's think: A Ferrers diagram is defined by a partition \u03bb = (\u03bb_1 \u2265 \u03bb_2 \u2265 ... \u2265 \u03bb_m). The shape consists of \u03bb_i squares in row i (from left). It is known that the minimal number of rectangles needed to partition it into rectangles is equal to the number of distinct part sizes in the partition (the number of distinct \u03bb_i). This is known as the \"rectangular decomposition of Young diagrams\". For the staircase partition \u03bb_i = n-i (for i=1..n-1), all \u03bb_i are distinct, so the number of distinct parts is n-1. So minimal rectangles = n-1. This is a known combinatorial theorem (maybe due to a simple greedy algorithm: each rectangle can reduce the number of distinct lengths by at most 1). So the lower bound holds.\n\nThus the upper triangle region requires at least n-1 rectangles. Similarly, the lower triangle region also requires at least n-1 rectangles. So total at least 2n-2.\n\nThus the minimal number of tiles needed is exactly 2n-2, where n = 2025. That would be 2*2025 - 2 = 4050 - 2 = 4048? Wait compute: 2*2025 = 4050. Subtract 2 gives 4048. Actually 2n - 2 = 2*2025 - 2 = 4050 - 2 = 4048. So answer: 4048 tiles.\n\nBut we must check whether any arrangement of missing squares (i.e., any permutation) could yield a lower number than 2n-2. The above lower bound argument used identity permutation (or any monotone permutation) that yields triangular shapes. But for other permutations, the shape of uncovered squares may be more mixed, maybe allowing larger rectangles that cross across more rows and columns. But still each rectangle cannot cross any zero. Could there be a permutation that reduces the minimal number of rectangles? The lower bound of 2n-2 may hold for any permutation, not just identity. Let's examine.\n\nWe need to prove that for any permutation pi, the complement of the permutation matrix (i.e., the set of allowed cells) can be partitioned into at least 2n-2 rectangles. Or equivalently, any tiling satisfying the constraints must use at least 2n-2 tiles.\n\nWe have an upper bound of 2n-2 using the column-star splitting approach (which works for any permutation? Actually the column-star splitting approach splits each column into up to two intervals: above and below the missing square in that column. For each column j, the missing square is at row i = pi^{-1}(j) (the row where pi(row) = j). So column j has a missing cell at row i. We can split column j into two rectangles: one covering rows 1..i-1 in column j, and another covering rows i+1..n in column j. So we get at most 2n rectangles, but for the first and last column maybe only one each (if missing is at top or bottom). So total number of rectangles is 2n - 2 (since there are exactly n columns, each contributes 2 intervals except the two columns where missing is at top or bottom? Actually for each column j, we split into two intervals unless the missing row is at either top (i=1) or bottom (i=n), in which case we have only one interval. But in general the missing row could be anywhere, so each column contributes 2 intervals, but we can reduce total count by 2 because we can combine the topmost intervals of some columns maybe? Wait we need to compute exactly: For each column j, missing row r_j = pi^{-1}(j). Then we have an interval above: rows 1..r_j-1 (if r_j>1) and below: rows r_j+1..n (if r_j<n). So each column yields up to 2 intervals. Summing for all columns gives sum_{j=1}^n ( [r_j>1] + [r_j<n] ) = sum_{j=1}^n (1 - [r_j=1] + 1 - [r_j=n]) = 2n - (#columns where r_j=1) - (#columns where r_j=n). Since each row is exactly one missing square; there is exactly one column where r_j=1 (the column where missing row is 1). Similarly exactly one column where r_j=n (the column where missing row is n). So we have exactly two columns where the missing row is at top or bottom. So total intervals = 2n - 2. So construction yields exactly 2n-2 rectangles for any permutation, regardless of shape. So upper bound holds.\n\nNow lower bound: Show at least 2n-2 rectangles needed. Let's attempt to prove that for any permutation pi, any tiling must contain at least 2n-2 rectangles. The reasoning may involve counting \"border squares\" or \"extremal rows/columns\".\n\nWe need to show that the number of rectangles is at least 2n-2. Equivalent to show that the sum of number of rectangles that intersect each row and each column must be at least something.\n\nOne approach: Consider the leftmost uncovered square in each row? Actually each row has exactly one missing square; we can consider the two sides of that missing square: left side (columns < pi(i)) and right side (columns > pi(i)). The missing square splits each row into left and right intervals (possibly empty). Similarly for each column, the missing square splits column into top and bottom intervals. A rectangle cannot cross the missing square in its row, because that would include the missing square. So a rectangle that includes row i can only be either entirely left of pi(i) or entirely right of pi(i). Because any rectangle covering row i and covering columns spanning both sides would include pi(i). Similarly, a rectangle covering column j must be either entirely above or below the missing row for that column.\n\nThus each rectangle can be categorized as either \"left side\" (covering rows and columns left of the missing square for each of its rows) or \"right side\". But more precisely: For each rectangle, consider the set of rows it covers: I. Let L =