ted by column sets. Let's try to find a 3 biclique partition for n=4.\n\nOne approach: Use three bicliques: J1 = {2,3,4}, I1 = {1} (cover row1 edges). J2 = {1,3,4}, I2 = {2} (row2). J3 = {1,2,?} Actually we need to cover row3 and row4 edges also. But we only have three bicliques; each row must appear in at least one biclique. But we must cover all edges for each row. For row3, edges are (3,1),(3,2),(3,4). Could we assign them to existing bicliques? Biclique I1 is row1 only, cannot cover row3. Biclique I2 is row2 only. So we need row3 to appear in at least one biclique, maybe I3 with some columns. But we only have three bicliques total, so I3 must cover row3 and row4 maybe. But we also need to cover row4 edges. Let's attempt:\n\nSet biclique A: rows {1,2}, columns {3,4}. This covers (1,3),(1,4),(2,3),(2,4). Good.\n\nSet biclique B: rows {3,4}, columns {1,2}. This covers (3,1),(3,2),(4,1),(4,2). Good.\n\nNow we need to cover edges (1,2),(2,1),(3,4),(4,3). That's four edges. Could we include them in a third biclique? Let's try biclique C: rows {1,2,3,4} (all rows), columns {???}. But we cannot include column 1 because that would include (1,1) missing; can't include column 2 because (2,2) missing; can't include column 3 because (3,3) missing; can't include column 4 because (4,4) missing. So we cannot have any column that is missing for any row present. So if we include all rows, column set must be empty. So not possible.\n\nAlternatively, we could adjust A and B to cover those remaining edges. For example, choose A: rows {1} columns {2,3,4} covering (1,2),(1,3),(1,4). B: rows {2,3,4} columns {1,4}? Wait we need to avoid missing squares: row2 missing column2, row3 missing column3, row4 missing column4. So B's column set cannot include 2,3,4 for rows 2,3,4 respectively. But if we include column2, row2 cannot be present. So B can be rows {2,3,4} columns {1} (just column1) covering (2,1),(3,1),(4,1). Then we still need to cover (2,3),(2,4),(3,2),(3,4),(4,2),(4,3). That's many edges. Hard to cover in only one more biclique.\n\nThus 3 seems impossible for n=4. But we found 4 works. So minimal for n=4 appears to be 4, which equals n? Actually n=4, minimal = 4? Let's verify if we can get 4 as lower bound: maybe minimal is n for even n? But n=3 minimal was 4, not 3. So pattern not n.\n\nWait for n=3 we found minimal 4, which is > n=3. For n=4 minimal 4 = n? Actually n=4 minimal 4 equals n. For n=2 minimal 2 = n. So pattern maybe minimal = n for even n, and minimal = n+1 for odd n? Let's test n=5 maybe minimal = 6? Let's try to find a partition for n=5 with 5 rectangles? Possibly not.\n\nWe need to find general formula.\n\nLet's test n=5 manually perhaps. Identity zeros at (i,i). We need to partition edges of K5 minus perfect matching. Could we partition into 5 bicliques? Let's try to generalize the construction we used for n=4: we used column stars (column j with all rows except row j). That gave us n bicliques (one per column). Actually we used 4 bicliques each being a column star, but we omitted column 4 star includes rows 1-3 only (excluding row4). Actually we used column stars for each column j, but each column star excludes the row j (since that edge is missing). So each column star includes all rows except the one whose missing column is that column. Thus each column star has n-1 rows, except maybe we can omit a column star? In our construction for n=4, we used all 4 columns: column 1 star covering rows 2,3,4; column 2 star covering rows 1,3,4; column 3 star covering rows 1,2,4; column 4 star covering rows 1,2,3. That's exactly 4 bicliques. So we partitioned all edges using exactly n column stars. Indeed, each edge (i,j) with i != j appears in column j star (since column star j includes all rows except row j). Since i != j, row i is included, so edge (i,j) is covered by column j star. And each edge appears only in that star because column j star is the only biclique containing column j (others may also contain column j, but they may also contain row i). But we can ensure partition if we only include column stars (each column j star covering all rows except j). Then each edge (i,j) appears exactly once (in column j star). So we have a partition of the graph into n bicliques: the column stars. That seems to be a valid partition for any n. But earlier we thought we needed 2n-2. Wait we have n bicliques! Let's verify: For n=3, column stars would be:\n\n- Column 1 star: rows 2,3 (edges (2,1),(3,1))\n- Column 2 star: rows 1,3 (edges (1,2),(3,2))\n- Column 3 star: rows 1,2 (edges (1,3),(2,3))\n\nThat's exactly 3 bicliques covering all 6 edges. But earlier we thought minimal for n=3 is 4. Let's check if the column star partition is valid: For each column j star, we include rows I_j = {i : i != j}. This set includes row j? No, we exclude row j. So each biclique includes all rows except the one whose missing column is that column. Since the missing squares are (i,i), each column star includes all rows except row j, avoiding the missing edge (j,j). The biclique includes all edges (i,j) for i != j. Indeed, those are all edges incident to column j (except the missing one). So the union of all column stars covers each edge exactly once, because each edge (i,j) belongs to column j star (and not to any other column star). So we have a perfect partition into n bicliques. So minimal number of tiles is at most n.\n\nBut earlier we thought for n=3 minimal is 4. Let's double-check with actual tiling: We need to partition the complement of the permutation matrix into rectangles. Using column stars translates to tiles that are vertical rectangles of width 1 (i.e., columns). For column j star, we have a rectangle covering rows = all rows except row j, column = j (so a vertical 1-column rectangle of height n-1). That's a tile of size (n-1) x 1. Since each tile must be a rectangle, this is fine. So for n=3, we have 3 tiles: column 1 tile covering rows 2-3, column 2 tile covering rows 1,3 (but rows 1 and 3 are not adjacent? Wait the rectangle must be contiguous rows; the tile must be a rectangle (contiguous rows and columns). The column star includes rows {2,3} for column 1: that's contiguous (rows 2-3). Good. For column 2 star includes rows {1,3} which are not contiguous (they are separated by row 2). That's not allowed as a single rectangle because there is a gap at row 2 where column 2 is missing (since (2,2) is missing). The tile would have to be a rectangle of rows 1-3, column 2, but that would include row2, column2 which is missing. So we cannot have a rectangle covering rows 1 and 3 only, leaving a hole at row2. So the column star for column 2 cannot be a single rectangle because the rows must be a contiguous interval. The set of rows {1,3} is not contiguous. So we need to split column 2 star into two rectangles: one for row 1, column 2 (1x1), and one for row 3, column 2 (1x1). That adds extra tiles. So the partition into column stars fails to respect contiguity of rows.\n\nThus the tile must be a rectangle where rows are a contiguous interval and columns are a contiguous interval. So each biclique I x J must have I and J as intervals (consecutive). That's a crucial additional constraint! Indeed, tile sides must be on grid lines, so the rectangle must be contiguous. So the biclique partition must be into \"interval bicliques\" (interval submatrices). That's more restrictive.\n\nThus the earlier construction of 2n-2 used intervals: rows intervals of length 1 (single row) and columns intervals of intervals (contiguous). For each row i, we made a rectangle covering row i and columns i+1 .. n (or columns i+1..n). That's an interval. Similarly for columns j we made rectangles covering rows j+1..n and column j (interval). So those are intervals. The column star for column j is not contiguous in rows unless we split at the missing row j. So to make intervals, we need to split column j star into two rectangles: one covering rows 1..j-1, column j; and another covering rows j+1..n, column j. That's 2(n-1) rectangles: each column yields two rectangles (except maybe first and last column? Actually column 1 yields rows 2..n, column 1 (contiguous) - it's one interval, because rows 1..0 doesn't exist. Similarly column n yields rows 1..n-1, column n, one interval. So total intervals = 2(n-1) - 2? Let's compute precisely.\n\nIf we use column intervals: For column j, we need to cover all rows except row j. So we can split into at most two intervals: rows 1..j-1 (if j > 1) and rows j+1..n (if j < n). So total number of rectangles from all columns = sum_{j=1}^n ( (j>1 ? 1 : 0) + (j<n ? 1 : 0) ) = (n-1) + (n-1) = 2n-2. Indeed, for j=1, we have only rows 2..n (one interval). For j=n, only rows 1..n-1 (one interval). For middle columns, two intervals. So total = 2n-2. This matches the earlier construction. So the 2n-2 construction is just the column-star decomposition with intervals.\n\nThus the minimal number of tiles is at most 2n-2. The question is: can we do even fewer using a mix of row intervals and column intervals, perhaps merging some intervals across columns to form larger rectangles that avoid missing squares? For n=4 we found a construction with 4 rectangles (which is less than 2n-2=6). But we need to verify if those rectangles are intervals. In our 4 rectangle construction for n=4, each rectangle was a column star (column j with rows all except j). However each column star had rows that are not contiguous except maybe column 1 star has rows 2,3,4 which are contiguous (rows 2-4). That's fine. Column 2 star has rows 1,3,4 => not contiguous (1 and 3,4). But we used column 2 star with rows {1,3,4}. That is not contiguous. Wait we listed C3: column 2 with rows {1,3,4}. That set is not contiguous because missing row 2. So we cannot have a rectangle that includes rows 1,3,4 but not row 2. So that construction is invalid for tiling with rectangles. Indeed, our rectangle must be contiguous rows. So the column-star decomposition into intervals (splitting into two intervals) yields 6 rectangles for n=4, not 4. Our earlier 4 biclique partition used non-interval sets, so it's invalid.\n\nThus the constraint that each rectangle must be axis-aligned contiguous intervals is crucial. So the lower bound may be 2n-2. But perhaps we can do better than 2n-2 by using rectangles that are not just columns or rows, but larger blocks that avoid zeros by being placed in \"staircase\" shape? For example, we can have a rectangle that covers rows i..j and columns p..q, as long as there is no zero within that submatrix. The zeros are at positions (k, pi(k)). So a rectangle must avoid any such positions. So for a given interval of rows I = [a,b] and columns J = [c,d], we must have for all k in I, pi(k) not in J. That is, the missing columns for rows in I must all lie outside J. So we need J to be a subset of columns that does not intersect pi(I). So if we choose I = some set of rows, we must avoid the columns that are images of those rows under pi. So the allowed columns are the complement of pi(I). For a given I, the maximum allowed J is the set of columns not in pi(I). In principle we could choose J to be all columns except pi(I), but J must be an interval. So we need an interval that avoids the set pi(I). Since pi(I) could be scattered, it's not possible to have a large interval that avoids them unless pi(I) is away from the interval. So the shape of pi matters: if pi is monotone (i.e., identity), then pi(I) = I (the same indices). So to avoid pi(I), we must choose J that does not intersect I, i.e., J must be either entirely left of I or entirely right of I. So for identity, any rectangle must be wholly above the diagonal, below the diagonal, or cross but avoid diagonal entries. Actually if I = rows [a,b] and J = columns [c,d] with c > b (i.e., J to the right of I), then pi(I) = I is not in J, so allowed. Similarly if d < a (J left of I). So rectangles can be placed either entirely above the main diagonal (i.e., rows less than columns) or below. So identity case yields rectangles either in upper triangular region or lower triangular region, but not crossing the diagonal. So the best we can do is partition the upper triangular region and lower triangular region separately. The upper region is a triangular shape, which can be partitioned into n-1 rectangles as we did (row intervals of length 1). But perhaps we could partition the upper triangle more efficiently using larger rectangles that cover multiple rows and columns while staying above diagonal. For example, rectangle covering rows 1..2, columns 3..4 (size 2x2) is allowed because rows 1,2 have missing columns 1,2 respectively; columns 3,4 are not among those missing columns, so okay. So we can combine rows and columns. So maybe we can reduce the number of rectangles for the upper triangle from n-1 to something smaller by using multi-row rectangles.\n\nSimilarly for lower triangle.\n\nThus the minimal tiling may be less than 2n-2. Indeed, for n=4, maybe we can tile the complement with 5 rectangles? Let's try to find a tiling for n=4 that uses 5 rectangles (or maybe 4?). We need to ensure each rectangle is contiguous rows and columns.\n\nLet's try to tile the upper triangle (i<j) of the 4x4 grid with rectangles. The region consists of squares:\n\nRow1: columns 2,3,4\nRow2: columns 3,4\nRow3: columns 4\nRow4: none (since i<j). That's a triangular shape. We can tile this region with rectangles:\n\n- We could take rectangle covering rows 1-3, columns 4 (i.e., column 4 from row1 to row3). That's contiguous rows 1..3 and column 4, covering squares (1,4),(2,4),(3,4). Good.\n\n- Then we need to cover squares (1,2),(1,3),(2,3). Could we cover (1,2) and (1,3) with rectangle row1, columns 2-3 (1x2). Then we need (2,3) left. Could we combine (2,3) with something else? Perhaps combine with (1,2) in a rectangle rows 1-2, columns 3? That would be (1,3),(2,3). But (1,3) already covered. Overlap not allowed. So we need separate rectangle for (2,3). That's a 1x1 tile. So total rectangles: column 4 (size 3x1), row1 columns 2-3 (size 1x2), and (2,3) (1x1). That's 3 rectangles for upper triangle. Similarly lower triangle (i>j) has squares:\n\nRow2: column1\nRow3: columns1,2\nRow4: columns1,2,3\n\nWe can tile similarly: column 1 rows2-4 (rows 2..4) covering (2,1),(3,1),(4,1). That's 1 rectangle. Then rectangle covering rows 3-4 columns 2 (size 2x1) covering (3,2),(4,2). Then rectangle covering (4,3) (1x1). That's another 3 rectangles. So total 6 rectangles. That matches the 2n-2 = 6.\n\nBut maybe we can do better by using larger rectangles that span both upper and lower triangle? That is not allowed because any rectangle crossing the diagonal would include a zero at the diagonal cell (i,i) if the rectangle includes that cell. But perhaps we can have a rectangle that includes both above and below diagonal but avoids the diagonal by having a \"hole\"? But rectangle cannot have a hole; it's contiguous. So any rectangle crossing the diagonal must include at least one diagonal cell (i,i) for some i that lies within the row interval and column interval. Since row interval includes i and column interval includes i, the cell (i,i) is included. So not allowed. Thus any rectangle must be wholly above or wholly below the diagonal (i.e., all rows indices less than column indices or greater). So the region is split into two disjoint triangular regions, each of which must be tiled separately.\n\nThus the problem reduces to tiling the upper triangular region (strictly above diagonal) and lower triangular region (strictly below diagonal) with axis-aligned rectangles that do not cross the diagonal. It's essentially a problem of partitioning a Young diagram shape (a right triangle) into rectangles.\n\nThis is reminiscent of the known combinatorial problem: partition the n x n board with one missing diagonal into rectangles. The minimal number of rectangles needed to partition a triangular half of a square (i.e., monotone region) is known to be n-1. Indeed, it's known that a triangular region of size n x n can be partitioned into n-1 rectangles (the \"staircase\" shape) and that this is optimal. For the upper triangle region (strictly above diagonal) which is a staircase shape: rows have decreasing lengths: row1 has n-1 squares, row2 has n-2 squares, ..., row_{n-1} has 1 square. This region is called a Young diagram of shape (n-1,n-2,...,1). The minimal number of rectangles needed to partition a Young diagram into rectangles (also called \"rectangle tiling\" of Ferrers diagram) is known to be the number of corners or something. There is known result: The minimal number of rectangles needed to partition a staircase shape of height n-1 is n-1. Indeed, one can partition it into n-1 rectangles by taking each row as a horizontal rectangle (like row i covering columns i+1..n). That's n-1 rectangles. Could we do fewer? Possibly not. Let's consider known result: The rectangle number of a Young diagram is equal to the size of its largest antichain in the poset of cells? Not sure.\n\nBut I think it's known that the staircase shape cannot be partitioned into fewer than n-1 rectangles. I recall a known combinatorial result: The \"staircase partition problem\" where the board is shape (n-1,n-2,...,1) and we ask for minimal number of rectangle partitions. The answer is n-1. This is because the shape has \"staircase\" with n-1 corners. Each rectangle can cover at most one corner? Or something like that. Let's consider: The shape has n-1 \"inner corners\" or \"exposed corners\" (the cells at (i,i+1) maybe?). Each rectangle can contain at most one of these \"stair steps\"? Possibly.\n\nAlternatively, we can prove lower bound using a \"monotone path\" argument: For each k from 1 to n-1, consider the cell at (k,n) (top right column) maybe? Not sure.\n\nLet's search memory: There's a known theorem by de Bruijn and van Ebben about partitioning Ferrers diagrams into rectangles, the minimal number equals the number of \"steps\" (i.e., number of distinct row lengths). The staircase shape has row lengths n-1, n-2, ..., 1, which are all distinct, so number of steps = n-1. So minimal rectangles = n-1. Indeed, for a Ferrers diagram (a Young diagram), the minimal number of rectangles needed to partition it is equal to the number of distinct row lengths. This is because each rectangle can cover a contiguous block of rows and columns, which correspond to a contiguous block of rows of same length? Something like that.\n\nLet's think: A Ferrers diagram is defined by a partition \u03bb = (\u03bb_1 \u2265 \u03bb_2 \u2265 ... \u2265 \u03bb_m). The shape consists of \u03bb_i squares in row i (from left). It is known that the minimal number of rectangles needed to partition it into rectangles is equal to the number of distinct part sizes in the partition (the number of distinct \u03bb_i). This is known as the \"rectangular decomposition of Young diagrams\". For the staircase partition \u03bb_i = n-i (for i=1..n-1), all \u03bb_i are distinct, so the number of distinct parts is n-1. So minimal rectangles = n-1. This is a known combinatorial theorem (maybe due to a simple greedy algorithm: each rectangle can reduce the number of distinct lengths by at most 1). So the lower bound holds.\n\nThus the upper triangle region requires at least n-1 rectangles. Similarly, the lower triangle region also requires at least n-1 rectangles. So total at least 2n-2.\n\nThus the minimal number of tiles needed is exactly 2n-2, where n = 2025. That would be 2*2025 - 2 = 4050 - 2 = 4048? Wait compute: 2*2025 = 4050. Subtract 2 gives 4048. Actually 2n - 2 = 2*2025 - 2 = 4050 - 2 = 4048. So answer: 4048 tiles.\n\nBut we must check whether any arrangement of missing squares (i.e., any permutation) could yield a lower number than 2n-2. The above lower bound argument used identity permutation (or any monotone permutation) that yields triangular shapes. But for other permutations, the shape of uncovered squares may be more mixed, maybe allowing larger rectangles that cross across more rows and columns. But still each rectangle cannot cross any zero. Could there be a permutation that reduces the minimal number of rectangles? The lower bound of 2n-2 may hold for any permutation, not just identity. Let's examine.\n\nWe need to prove that for any permutation pi, the complement of the permutation matrix (i.e., the set of allowed cells) can be partitioned into at least 2n-2 rectangles. Or equivalently, any tiling satisfying the constraints must use at least 2n-2 tiles.\n\nWe have an upper bound of 2n-2 using the column-star splitting approach (which works for any permutation? Actually the column-star splitting approach splits each column into up to two intervals: above and below the missing square in that column. For each column j, the missing square is at row i = pi^{-1}(j) (the row where pi(row) = j). So column j has a missing cell at row i. We can split column j into two rectangles: one covering rows 1..i-1 in column j, and another covering rows i+1..n in column j. So we get at most 2n rectangles, but for the first and last column maybe only one each (if missing is at top or bottom). So total number of rectangles is 2n - 2 (since there are exactly n columns, each contributes 2 intervals except the two columns where missing is at top or bottom? Actually for each column j, we split into two intervals unless the missing row is at either top (i=1) or bottom (i=n), in which case we have only one interval. But in general the missing row could be anywhere, so each column contributes 2 intervals, but we can reduce total count by 2 because we can combine the topmost intervals of some columns maybe? Wait we need to compute exactly: For each column j, missing row r_j = pi^{-1}(j). Then we have an interval above: rows 1..r_j-1 (if r_j>1) and below: rows r_j+1..n (if r_j<n). So each column yields up to 2 intervals. Summing for all columns gives sum_{j=1}^n ( [r_j>1] + [r_j<n] ) = sum_{j=1}^n (1 - [r_j=1] + 1 - [r_j=n]) = 2n - (#columns where r_j=1) - (#columns where r_j=n). Since each row is exactly one missing square; there is exactly one column where r_j=1 (the column where missing row is 1). Similarly exactly one column where r_j=n (the column where missing row is n). So we have exactly two columns where the missing row is at top or bottom. So total intervals = 2n - 2. So construction yields exactly 2n-2 rectangles for any permutation, regardless of shape. So upper bound holds.\n\nNow lower bound: Show at least 2n-2 rectangles needed. Let's attempt to prove that for any permutation pi, any tiling must contain at least 2n-2 rectangles. The reasoning may involve counting \"border squares\" or \"extremal rows/columns\".\n\nWe need to show that the number of rectangles is at least 2n-2. Equivalent to show that the sum of number of rectangles that intersect each row and each column must be at least something.\n\nOne approach: Consider the leftmost uncovered square in each row? Actually each row has exactly one missing square; we can consider the two sides of that missing square: left side (columns < pi(i)) and right side (columns > pi(i)). The missing square splits each row into left and right intervals (possibly empty). Similarly for each column, the missing square splits column into top and bottom intervals. A rectangle cannot cross the missing square in its row, because that would include the missing square. So a rectangle that includes row i can only be either entirely left of pi(i) or entirely right of pi(i). Because any rectangle covering row i and covering columns spanning both sides would include pi(i). Similarly, a rectangle covering column j must be either entirely above or below the missing row for that column.\n\nThus each rectangle can be categorized as either \"left side\" (covering rows and columns left of the missing square for each of its rows) or \"right side\". But more precisely: For each rectangle, consider the set of rows it covers: I. Let L = min_{