two rectangles: (1-2) and (4). So total rectangles from column splits: column1: 2, column2:1, column3:1, column4:2 => total 6 rectangles. So same as identity case.\n\nBut perhaps we can combine across columns to reduce count. For example, we could have a rectangle covering rows 2-4 and columns 2-3 (i.e., a 3x2 block) that includes squares (2,2),(2,3),(3,2),(3,3),(4,2),(4,3). However (4,3) is missing (since missing at (4,3)!). So cannot include column 3 rows 4. So that rectangle is invalid. Maybe we can cover rows 2-3, columns 2-4? That would include (3,4) missing. So no.\n\nLet's systematically search for possible rectangles that cover many squares.\n\nThe missing squares are at (1,2), (2,1), (3,4), (4,3). So the pattern is two \"cross swaps\". This permutation has two 2-cycles. The complement graph is symmetric.\n\nNow, we need to partition the complement. The complement shape is the full board minus these four squares. This shape is not a simple triangular shape; it's more \"dense\". Might allow larger rectangles.\n\nPotential rectangle: rows 1-2, columns 3-4. This covers squares (1,3),(1,4),(2,3),(2,4). All are present (since missing squares are only column2 for row1 and column1 for row2). So this rectangle is allowed (size 2x2). Good.\n\nSimilarly rectangle rows 3-4, columns 1-2 covers squares (3,1),(3,2),(4,1),(4,2). All present (missing squares are column4 for row3 and column3 for row4). So this rectangle is allowed (size 2x2). Good.\n\nNow remaining squares: (1,1) (present), (1,3?) Already covered? Wait (1,3) is in rectangle rows1-2, cols3-4, so covered. (1,4) covered. (2,2) present? Row2 col2 present. (2,1) missing. (2,3) covered. (2,4) covered. (3,3) present? Row3 col3 present. (3,1) covered. (3,2) covered. (3,4) missing. (4,4) present. (4,3) missing. (4,1) covered. (4,2) covered.\n\nLeftover squares after covering the two 2x2 blocks:\n\n- Square (1,1)\n- Square (2,2)\n- Square (3,3)\n- Square (4,4)\n\nThese are the main diagonal squares (i,i). They are all