arts across rows produce a shape that is a Young diagram (maybe not monotone decreasing lengths). Similarly right parts produce another shape. We can argue that each of these shapes requires at least n-1 rectangles to partition, regardless of permutation. Let's examine.\n\nConsider the left side of each missing square: For each row i, define L_i = {columns j < pi(i)}; the left part of row i. Let L_i be possibly empty. The collection of these left parts across rows forms a shape that is a Young diagram rotated? Actually if we sort rows by the missing column index pi(i), we can see that the sizes of L_i are monotone decreasing? Not necessarily: pi(i) could be any permutation, so the left side lengths can be arbitrary. But we can consider the shape formed by all squares left of missing squares: the set S = {(i,j) : j < pi(i)}. This shape is exactly the set of squares that are to the left of the missing square in each row. Similarly the right side shape T = {(i,j) : j > pi(i)}. These two shapes partition the complement (since any square must be either left or right of the missing square in its row). So any rectangle that covers squares from both left and right sides of a row is impossible because it would cross the missing square. So each rectangle must be wholly within S or wholly within T. So we can treat the problem as tiling S and T separately. Since S and T are each shapes with at most n-1 rows (maybe some rows have size 0). But they are \"Ferrers diagrams\"? Let's examine S.\n\nDefine for each row i, the left part length L_i = pi(i)-1 (since columns 1..pi(i)-1). So L_i values are a permutation of numbers 0..n-1 (since pi(i) runs over 1..n). Indeed, the multiset {L_i} = {0,1,2,...,n-1} because pi(i) runs over all values. So there is exactly one row with left part length 0 (the row where pi(i)=1), one row with left part length 1 (pi(i)=2), etc. So the left side shape S consists of rows of lengths 0,1,...,n-1, each length occurring exactly once. However the rows are not necessarily sorted by length; they appear in arbitrary order. So S is a Ferrers diagram after permuting rows. But the shape S is a \"permuted\" Young diagram: rows of lengths 0..n-1 in some order.\n\nNow we ask: what is the minimal number of axis-aligned rectangles needed to partition S? Since the rows can be in any order, perhaps we can partition S more efficiently than n-1? We need to consider that rectangles must be contiguous rows and columns. Because rows are not sorted by length, you might be able to combine rows of varying lengths into rectangles that cover columns up to the smallest length among them, etc.\n\nBut note that any rectangle in S must be comprised of rows I and columns J such that for each row i in I, J \u2286 [1, pi(i)-1]. Since pi(i)-1 = L_i is the max column index allowed for that row (since columns are left of missing square). So J must be a subset of the intersection of intervals [1, L_i] across rows i in I. Intersection of intervals [1, L_i] = [1, min_{i\u2208I} L_i]. So any rectangle in S can be defined by a set of rows I and a column interval J = [1, c] where c \u2264 min_{i\u2208I} L_i. So rectangles are anchored at column 1 (they always start at column 1) and extend to some column c \u2264 min(L_i). Because they must be leftmost contiguous block from column 1 onward. Since J must be contiguous and start at column 1 maybe not necessarily; could start at column a>1 if all rows have L_i >= a, but then we would be missing the leftmost columns for those rows (which might be covered by other rectangles). However due to the shape, any rectangle can be placed somewhere within the left region, not necessarily anchored at column 1. For a given row i with left length L_i, the allowed columns are 1..L_i. So a rectangle can be placed in columns a..b where a >= 1 and b \u2264 L_i for all rows i in I. So the intersection of allowed intervals for rows is [1, min L_i]. So any rectangle's column interval must be a subinterval of [1, min L_i]. So it can be placed anywhere inside that, but can't exceed that min.\n\nThus the shape S is like a set of rows of varying lengths, each row being a left-justified interval from column 1 to L_i. So S is a \"Ferrers board\" but rows may not be monotonic: the lengths are in arbitrary order. However the shape's columns also have varying heights: column j is present in those rows i where L_i \u2265 j, i.e., rows where pi(i) > j. Since pi is a permutation, column j has exactly n-j rows (since pi(i) > j means pi(i) \u2208 {j+1,...,n}, which are n-j values). So column j has exactly n-j rows occupied (i.e., squares in S). So the column heights are monotonic decreasing: column 1 has n-1 rows (since all rows except the one with pi(i)=1 have left part length >=1). Actually column 1 is present in all rows where pi(i) > 1, i.e., all rows except the one where pi(i)=1. So column 1 has n-1 squares. Column 2 has n-2 squares (all rows except those where pi(i) <=2). In general, column j has n-j squares. So the shape S is a \"standard Young diagram\" shape of partition (n-1,n-2,...,1) after possibly permuting rows? But the shape is exactly the same as the upper triangular shape when rows are sorted by L_i decreasing. However the rows are not sorted, but the shape as a set of squares is exactly the same as the upper triangular region: the set of squares (i,j) such that j < pi(i). Since pi is a permutation, the condition j < pi(i) is equivalent to (i,j) with j < pi(i). But if we reorder rows according to pi(i), we get the shape of a Young diagram. However the actual arrangement of squares in the grid is not contiguous rows; the rows are in original order, but the shape S is still a set of squares that is a \"Ferrers diagram\" but possibly \"skewed\". However for tiling with rectangles, the order of rows matters because rectangles must be contiguous in original row ordering. So the shape may be more complicated.\n\nNevertheless, we can consider the \"profile\" of S: For each row i, we have left segment of length L_i. The lengths L_i are a permutation of 0..n-1. So the shape is essentially a \"staircase\" but rows are permuted. This shape is known as a \"permutation diagram\" for the inverse permutation? Actually the set of points (i, pi(i)) is the permutation matrix; the squares to the left of the points form a Young diagram shape after sorting rows by pi(i). But after permuting rows, the shape is a \"permutation diagram\" which is a 0-1 matrix with ones left of the permutation points and zeros elsewhere. This is known as a \"permutation matrix's lower-left region\". The shape is a \"matrix of shape of a permutation\". It is known that the minimal number of rectangles needed to partition such a shape is exactly n-1, regardless of row ordering. Let's verify.\n\nThe shape S is a \"board\" that is the complement of the permutation pattern above the main diagonal after applying a permutation of rows. The shape is known to be a \"Ferrers board\" after sorting rows appropriately; but before sorting, it's a \"permutation board\". However, the minimal number of rectangles needed to partition it might be n-1 because each rectangle can cover at most one \"corner\" of the shape? Let's examine.\n\nConsider the shape S: it's a monotone shape: If you look at any column j, the rows that have squares in that column are exactly those rows i where L_i >= j, i.e., pi(i) > j. Since pi(i) > j is a set of rows that is a permutation of some numbers. This set is not necessarily contiguous in row order: it could be any subset of rows of size n-j. So column j squares may be scattered across rows, not forming a contiguous vertical strip. So shape S is not necessarily a Young diagram; it's more like a \"rook diagram\".\n\nThus tiling S with rectangles may be more complex. However we can still tile S by splitting each column into at most two intervals (similar to earlier column splitting). Because for column j, the missing square is at row i where pi(i) = j. So column j has a missing square at row i. So the present squares in column j are rows above i (1..i-1) and rows below i (i+1..n). So they are at most two intervals. So we can partition S into at most 2n-2 rectangles (the same 2n-2 construction). But we need a lower bound.\n\nThe earlier lower bound for identity case used distinct row lengths argument. But for arbitrary permutation, the shape S may be more \"jagged\". Could it be partitioned into fewer rectangles than n-1? Possibly yes, because rows of varying lengths may allow merging. Let's test with a small n=3 permutation (identity). We found minimal = 4 overall (so each side needed 2 rectangles). That's n-1 = 2 for each side. Could there be a permutation where left side shape can be tiled with only 1 rectangle? For n=3, left side lengths are L_i = pi(i)-1. The multiset is {0,1,2}. Could we tile left side shape with only 1 rectangle? That would require a rectangle covering all left side squares. The left side squares are:\n\n- Row with L_i=0: no squares.\n- Row with L_i=1: column 1.\n- Row with L_i=2: columns 1-2.\n\nIf the rows with L_i=1 and L_i=2 are contiguous (i.e., appear consecutively in original ordering), then we could have a rectangle covering rows of those two rows and columns 1-2? But the row with L_i=1 only has column 1; column 2 is missing in that row (since L_i=1). So rectangle rows i1,i2 columns 1-2 would include a missing square at (row with L_i=1, col2). So not allowed. So you can't cover left side with a single rectangle. So need at least 2 rectangles. So for n=3 left side minimal = 2.\n\nFor n=4, left side lengths are {0,1,2,3}. Could we tile left side shape with 2 rectangles? Possibly. Let's try to construct a permutation where left side shape can be tiled with 2 rectangles. For that, we need to partition the left side squares into 2 rectangles. Since there are 6 squares left side (sum of lengths = 0+1+2+3 = 6). Each rectangle must be contiguous rows and columns. Could we have rectangle covering rows with L_i >=2 and 