              "text": "We need to understand the problem: We have a 2025 x 2025 grid (so N = 2025). We need to place rectangular tiles (axis-aligned, sides on grid lines), covering unit squares with no overlap (each unit square covered by at most one tile). There is exactly one uncovered unit square in each row, and exactly one uncovered unit square in each column. So we have a set of uncovered squares that forms a permutation matrix: exactly one empty square per row and column, thus exactly N uncovered squares total (since each row has one missing, each column has one missing; they must be distinct squares, else a row and column would share same missing square? Actually could they be the same? We have N rows and N columns, each row has a missing square somewhere; each column also has a missing square somewhere. If we think of missing squares coordinates (i, j_i), then each column j appears exactly once as well. So the missing squares are a permutation: there is a bijection between rows and columns: the set of uncovered squares is a set of N positions, one per row, one per column. So it's a permutation matrix.\n\nGoal: tile the rest of the grid (N^2 - N squares) with axis-aligned rectangles, no overlaps, covering all squares except those N missing squares. Tiles can be of varying sizes. We want to minimize the number of tiles used. Equivalent: maximize the size (area) of each tile, but subject to constraints that each tile cannot include any missing square (since uncovered squares cannot be covered). Also each tile must be a rectangle covering some contiguous block of squares.\n\nWe need to find the minimal number of rectangles needed to partition the grid minus a permutation matrix.\n\nThis is reminiscent of the problem of covering a board with rectangles given forbidden cells; we want minimal number of rectangles = minimal partition of the complement of a permutation matrix into axis-aligned rectangles (with integer coordinates). This is essentially a combinatorial problem related to \"monotone matrix decomposition\", \"grid decomposition\", \"Young diagram\"? There is known result: For an n x n board with a rook placement (i.e., a permutation matrix), the minimum number of axis-aligned rectangles needed to partition the board into rectangles not containing any rook is n (or something like 2n-1?). Let's think.\n\nBut we need to consider general permutations: the minimal number of rectangles needed to partition the complement of a permutation matrix might depend on the permutation. For some permutations, you can partition into n rectangles (like along rows or columns avoiding the missing squares). But can we achieve fewer? Let's try small cases.\n\nIf N=1, trivial: no tile needed? Actually the board is 1x1, missing square is that only square, so no tile needed: min tiles = 0.\n\nIf N=2, we have a 2x2 board. There are two missing squares: one per row, one per column. Permutation possibilities: identity (missing squares at (1,1) and (2,2)). The complement consists of squares (1,2) and (2,1). These are not adjacent (they are diagonal). They cannot form a rectangle because a rectangle covering both would also include (1,1) and (2,2) which are missing. So each must be its own 1x1 tile. So min tiles = 2. Could we do better? No. So min tiles = N? Here N=2, tiles=2.\n\nIf N=2 with other permutation (swap? Actually identity is the only permutation for N=2? Wait 2 permutations: identity (1,1), (2,2) and swap (1,2), (2,1). Both have same pattern: missing squares are diagonal. Complement squares are the other diagonal. Same result: need 2 tiles.\n\nIf N=3, consider identity permutation: missing squares at (1,1), (2,2), (3,3). Complement squares are all other 6 squares. Can we partition complement into 3 tiles? Possibly: Consider tile covering row 1 columns 2-3 (size 1x2), tile covering row 2 columns 1 and 3? Not contiguous: can't cover columns 1 and 3 without covering column 2 which is missing at (2,2). So can't. Let's think: The complement can be partitioned into 3 rectangles: For each row i, we can take the contiguous segment of squares to the right of the missing square (or left of) but that would be contiguous per row but not across rows. However we can also combine across rows where missing squares are aligned in columns.\n\nGeneral idea: For each column j, there is exactly one missing square. So we can partition the complement into \"non-intersecting\" rectangles that are maximal contiguous blocks without missing squares. This is like partition by monotone paths.\n\nWe need find minimal number of rectangles. This is known as the \"rectangular partition of a permutation matrix complement\". There might be known result: the minimal number of rectangles needed equals the size of a minimal chain decomposition in the poset associated to the permutation. Or something like the size of a maximum antichain. Let's consider known combinatorial concept: Permutation matrix corresponds to a partial order known as the permutation poset (the points are (i, pi(i)). The complement can be partitioned into increasing and decreasing sequences? Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, think of each tile as a rectangle that doesn't contain any missing squares. So the missing squares act as obstacles dividing the board into rectangular regions. The minimal number of rectangles needed to cover the whole board minus obstacles is the minimal number of rectangles needed to partition a grid with \"holes\". This is like a \"rectangular partition problem\" which is NP-hard in general. But for a permutation matrix, maybe there's a simple formula.\n\nActually, there might be a known result: The minimal number of rectangles needed to partition the complement of a permutation matrix of size n is exactly n (the number of missing squares). Because each missing square forces a \"cut\" that splits the board in such a way that each rectangle can avoid at most one missing square? But need to check.\n\nLet's test for n=3. Could we partition complement into 3 rectangles? Let's try identity. The missing squares are diagonal. The complement consists of all squares except diagonal. Visualize 3x3 board:\n\nRow1: col1 missing, col2 present, col3 present.\nRow2: col1 present, col2 missing, col3 present.\nRow3: col1 present, col2 present, col3 missing.\n\nWe need to partition the 6 squares into rectangles. Potential rectangles:\n- Top right 2x1 rectangle: squares (1,2)-(1,3) contiguous horizontally: that's row1 columns 2-3 (size 1x2).\n- Bottom left 2x1 rectangle: row3 columns 1-2 (size 1x2).\nNow left middle and right middle squares: (2,1) and (2,3). They are not adjacent. Could we combine them with other squares? Not with row1 or row3 because that would include missing squares. So they each need separate 1x1 tiles. So total rectangles = 4. That's >3.\n\nCould there be arrangement with 3 rectangles? Let's try alternative partition: Use vertical rectangles: column1 rows 2-3 (size 2x1) covering squares (2,1) and (3,1). Not possible because (3,1) is present? Yes it's present (missing at (3,3)). So we can have rectangle covering rows 2-3 in column1 (2x1). Similarly, column3 rows 1-2 (size 2x1) covering squares (1,3) and (2,3). That's two rectangles. Then remaining squares: (1,2) and (3,2). They are not adjacent, so separate. So again 4.\n\nThus minimal seems to be 4 for n=3 (maybe). Let's check other permutations. Permutation (1,2), (2,1), (3,3). So missing squares at (1,2),(2,1),(3,3). Let's draw board:\n\nRow1: col1 present, col2 missing, col3 present.\nRow2: col1 missing, col2 present, col3 present.\nRow3: col1 present, col2 present, col3 missing.\n\nNow complement squares: (1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(2,3),(3,1),(3,2). Could we partition into 3 rectangles? Perhaps we can do one rectangle covering top-left 2x2 block except missing squares? But that includes (1,2) missing and (2,1) missing; can't. Let's attempt to see if 3 is possible. Maybe no; perhaps minimal number is 4 for all permutations for n=3. Let's test using known result: minimal number of rectangles needed to partition complement of a permutation matrix is equal to the size of a maximum antichain in the permutation poset, which for N=3 is 2? Actually antichain size can be 2? Eh.\n\nBut we need not compute exact minimal number for n=3; we need for n=2025. The answer presumably is something like 2*2025 - 1 = 4049? Or maybe 2*2025 - k? Let's think.\n\nWe need to find minimal number of rectangles covering all but N squares such that each row and column has exactly one uncovered square. Equivalent to partitioning the board into rectangles that avoid those missing squares.\n\nWe can think of constructing a tiling with some number of rectangles, maybe using large rectangles that encompass many squares while carefully avoiding missing squares. The missing squares are like rooks placed such that each row and column has exactly one. So they are a permutation matrix. The complement is the board minus rooks.\n\nWe want to partition the board into rectangles that do not contain any rook. This is similar to partitioning the board into \"rook-free rectangles\". The minimal number of such rectangles needed to cover the board is known as the \"rook covering number\" or something? Let's recall known combinatorial result: In an n x n board with a permutation of rooks, the board can be partitioned into n rectangles each of which is a \"staircase\" shape? Actually not.\n\nAlternatively, we can think of constructing a tiling based on the permutation's cycles. For each cycle in the permutation, we can tile the board using a certain number of rectangles. Possibly the minimal number equals N + number of cycles? Or something.\n\nLet's try to derive a general lower bound: Each tile is a rectangle. For each missing square, it must be at the \"corner\" of some rectangle? Actually each missing square lies at the intersection of a row and column. Since each row has exactly one missing square, the covering rectangles in that row cannot cross that column. Similarly for each column. So the missing squares act as \"cuts\". If we project the missing squares onto the grid lines, they partition the board into rectangular \"connected components\" if we consider adjacency of squares horizontally/vertically. But due to missing squares being isolated points, the complement is still a single connected region (maybe not). But the missing squares are points that block adjacency. However, they don't block connectivity across corners? Actually adjacency is 4-directional: squares share edges. Missing squares block adjacency across that square, but you can go around them.\n\nBut the rectangles we can place must not include missing squares, but they can cross over rows/columns that contain missing squares in other rows/columns as long as they don't include that specific missing cell. For example, a rectangle can span many rows, and many columns, but it must avoid any missing squares inside its interior. So the rectangle's region must be a submatrix that contains no missing squares. So each rectangle corresponds to a submatrix of the board that is \"full\" (no missing squares). So the problem reduces to partition the full submatrix of the board (the complement of a permutation matrix) into a minimal number of full submatrices (sub-rectangles) that are pairwise disjoint and cover all 1's (present squares). The missing squares are zeros.\n\nThus we need to cover all entries of the n x n matrix with ones (except zeros at permutation positions) with a set of all-ones submatrices, each submatrix being a rectangular block (contiguous rows and columns) that does not contain any zero. This is exactly the problem of partitioning a 0-1 matrix with a single zero per row and column into all-ones submatrices.\n\nWe want minimal number of submatrices.\n\nThis is reminiscent of the concept of \"monochromatic rectangle covering number\" for matrices, which is the minimum number of monochromatic submatrices needed to cover all 1's without covering any 0's. This is also known as the \"covering number\" of the matrix. For a permutation matrix, the covering number is known to be n, because each 1 is isolated? Wait no, the matrix here is the complement of a permutation matrix, which is a matrix with zeros on the permutation positions and ones elsewhere. The covering number for the ones might be something else. For a matrix of all ones except a permutation of zeros, the covering number of the ones (by all-ones submatrices) likely is n, because each zero creates a need for a cut across both its row and column, perhaps.\n\nBut we need to confirm.\n\nLet's define the complement matrix M (size n x n) where M[i][j] = 1 if (i,j) is not a missing square, else M[i][j] = 0. So M is a matrix of ones with zeros at positions (i, pi(i)). We want to partition the set of 1's into rectangles that contain only 1's. Equivalent to covering all 1's with disjoint all-ones rectangles.\n\nWe can think of each rectangle corresponds to a pair of intervals of rows and columns: rows in I, columns in J, such that for all i in I, j in J, M[i][j] = 1 (i.e., no zero inside). That means for any zero (i, pi(i)), either i not in I or pi(i) not in J.\n\nThus a rectangle cannot contain any zero, i.e., it must avoid the rows and columns that intersect at a zero in a certain way.\n\nNow, what is minimal number of rectangles needed? Let's denote f(n) minimal number for any permutation (worst-case) or we can choose a permutation to minimize f? The problem likely asks for the minimum number of tiles needed overall, i.e., we can choose the placement of uncovered squares (the permutation) to minimize number of tiles. So we can arrange the uncovered squares arbitrarily as we like (subject to exactly one per row and column). So we can choose a permutation that yields the smallest possible number of rectangles needed to tile the rest.\n\nSo we need to find the optimal arrangement of uncovered squares that yields minimal number of rectangles. Then find the minimal number of rectangles needed for that arrangement. So it's an optimization.\n\nThus we can decide the permutation pi to minimize tile count. So we need to find the minimal possible number of rectangles covering the complement of a permutation matrix for some permutation.\n\nObservation: If we choose the permutation such that the missing squares lie all in the same diagonal (i,i) (identity), we might get many small gaps. But maybe there is a better arrangement, like all missing squares lie on a single row? Not allowed because each row must have exactly one missing square, each column exactly one, so it's a permutation. So we cannot cluster them.\n\nBut perhaps we can choose a permutation that yields a structure where the complement can be tiled with relatively few large rectangles. For instance, if the permutation is the \"reverse\" (i, n+1-i), the zeros are on anti-diagonal, still similar.\n\nMaybe we can choose a permutation that is a single long cycle? But the pattern of zeros is just a set of points; the cycle structure may affect the shape of the complement's partition.\n\nOne approach: Partition the board into 2n-1 rectangles: we can take all rows above the diagonal as one big rectangle? Actually consider the complement of identity zeros. The region of squares above the main diagonal (i < j) is a triangular region of area n(n-1)/2. Similarly region below diagonal (i > j). Both are full of 1's. But each triangular region is not a rectangle; it's a triangular shape. However, we could tile each triangular region with n-1 rectangles? For the region above diagonal, we can tile it by rectangles that are each row i covering columns i+1..n. That's n-1 rectangles (one per row, except the last row). Similarly region below diagonal yields another n-1 rectangles (one per column?). Wait below diagonal: squares (i > j) can be tiled by columns: for each column j, rows j+1..n. That's n-1 rectangles. So total rectangles = 2(n-1). Plus we also have to cover the squares on the diagonal? The diagonal squares are missing (zeros). So not needed. So tiling uses 2(n-1) rectangles. That's 2n-2 rectangles. For n=3, this yields 4 rectangles (2*2=4). Indeed we found minimal was 4. For n=2, 2*1=2, matches. For n=1, 0.\n\nThus a simple construction yields 2n-2 tiles. Is this minimal? Could we do fewer? For n=3 we suspect minimal is 4, which equals 2n-2 = 4. So maybe minimal is 2n-2 for all n>1. Let's test n=4.\n\nConstruct identity permutation. Upper region: for each row i from 1 to n-1, tile rectangle covering columns i+1..n (size 1 x (n-i)). That's n-1 rectangles. Lower region: for each column j from 2 to n, tile rectangle covering rows j..n (size (n-j+1) x 1). That's n-1 rectangles. So total 2n-2 = 6 rectangles for n=4.\n\nBut maybe we can do better than 6? Let's try to find a tiling with 5 rectangles? Might be possible with a different permutation. Let's attempt to search for minimal for small n to see pattern.\n\nWe can try to brute force for n up to maybe 5 or 6 to see minimal number of rectangles for some permutation. Since this is a reasoning exercise, we can simulate mentally or logically.\n\nBut we need to verify lower bound: can we prove at least 2n-2 rectangles are needed? Possibly yes. Let's think: For each row, the missing square creates a \"break\" in that row: the row is split into at most 2 contiguous segments (left of missing square, right of missing square). Similarly for each column. The union of all rectangles must cover all these segments. Each rectangle that crosses a missing square's row cannot cross that column, so each rectangle's row interval cannot include a missing square column unless the rectangle's column interval excludes that column. So each rectangle can be classified as either lying completely to the left or right of a missing square for each row it covers. This leads to constraints.\n\nAlternatively, think of the bipartite graph representation: The missing squares correspond to edges of a perfect matching in a complete bipartite graph K_{n,n} (rows vs columns). The complement of the matching is a complete bipartite graph minus the matching edges. We want to cover all edges of this bipartite graph (i.e., the present squares) with a set of complete bipartite subgraphs (i.e., rectangles). A rectangle corresponds to a set of rows I and columns J such that all edges between I and J are present (i.e., no missing square). This is exactly a complete bipartite subgraph that does not contain any missing edge (i.e., not containing any edge of the matching). So we want to partition the edge set of K_{n,n} \\ M (where M is a perfect matching) into complete bipartite subgraphs (bicliques) that are edge-disjoint and cover all edges. This is a biclique partition problem.\n\nThe minimal number of bicliques needed to partition the complement of a perfect matching is known? I recall a known result: The biclique partition number of K_{n,n} minus a perfect matching is exactly 2n-2. Indeed, there is a known result: The bipartite graph K_{n,n} minus a perfect matching has biclique partition number equal to n + \\lceil \\log_2 n \\rceil? Not sure. Let's search memory: There's a known parameter called the \"biclique partition number\" (or \"Boolean rank\") of the adjacency matrix of the bipartite graph. For the graph K_{n,n} minus a perfect matching (i.e., the complement of the identity matrix), its Boolean rank is known to be ceil(log_2 n) + 1? Actually not.\n\nWait, the Boolean rank of a matrix is the minimum number r such that the matrix can be expressed as a Boolean sum (OR) of r rank-1 matrices. This is akin to covering by rectangles allowing overlaps (not necessarily partition). But we need partition (disjoint rectangles) not just covering. So it's a different concept.\n\nNevertheless, there is known result: The edge set of K_{n,n} minus a perfect matching can be partitioned into 2n-2 complete bipartite subgraphs, and this is optimal. I think I've seen this in combinatorics. Let's verify.\n\nConsider the simple construction: For each i from 1 to n-1, define biclique B_i = ({i}, {i+1,...,n}) (i.e., row i to columns > i). That's a star from row i to columns to the right. That's n-1 bicliques. Similarly, for each j from 2 to n, define B'_j = ({j,...,n}, {j}) (i.e., rows >= j to column j). That's n-1 bicliques. Together they cover all edges except those of the matching: edges (i,i) are missing, and all other edges appear exactly once. Indeed, each edge (i,j) with i<j appears in B_i (row i to columns >i). Edge (i,j) with i>j appears in B'_j (rows >j to column j). Edge (i,i) is missing. So we partition the edge set into 2n-2 bicliques. So we have a partition using 2n-2 rectangles.\n\nNow we need to prove no partition can use fewer rectangles. Let's attempt to prove lower bound: Consider the bipartite graph G = K_{n,n} \\ M. The bipartite adjacency matrix has exactly n^2 - n ones. Each biclique (rectangle) can be thought of as a complete bipartite subgraph. Suppose we have a partition into t bicliques. For each missing edge (i,i) (or pi(i) in general), note that any biclique that includes row i must exclude column pi(i), else it would contain the missing edge. Similarly any biclique that includes column pi(i) must exclude row i. So for each i, the set of bicliques covering row i (i.e., those whose row set includes i) must be such that each of those bicliques' column sets avoid pi(i). Similarly for column pi(i). So each row i participates in some number of bicliques, each of which uses some columns not containing pi(i). So each row i must be covered by at least something. In the partition, each edge (i,j) appears exactly once. So each row i has n-1 edges (to all columns except pi(i)). Those edges are partitioned among bicliques that contain row i. Each such biclique that contains row i includes some set of columns (maybe multiple). So the total number of edges from row i covered by a biclique is size of its column set. Thus the number of bicliques that contain row i is at least the number of \"blocks\" needed to cover the columns except pi(i) such that each block is a subset of columns that appear together in a biclique with row i. Since each biclique containing row i can have any subset of columns (as long as they avoid pi(i) and also avoid zeros for other rows in that biclique). But we need to ensure that for any other row i' in the same biclique, the column set also avoids pi(i'). So the column set for a biclique must be a subset of columns that avoid the missing columns for all rows in that biclique. So the column set must be a subset of columns not equal to any pi(i') for any row i' in the biclique. So there is a global restriction.\n\nThus to minimize number of bicliques, we may want to combine many rows together in a biclique, but they must have a common set of columns that avoid all missing columns for those rows. This is reminiscent of the condition that the rows in a biclique must correspond to a set of rows I such that the missing columns for those rows are all distinct and not in the column set J. So J must be a subset of columns that are not among {pi(i) : i in I}. So we can choose J = complement of {pi(i) : i in I} within the column set? But also J must be a subset that yields all edges between I and J being present (they are, because zeros only at (i, pi(i)), and pi(i) not in J). So any I and J with J disjoint from pi(I) yields a complete bipartite subgraph. So we can combine many rows into a biclique as long as we don't include any column that is the missing column for any row in I. So the column set must avoid pi(I).\n\nThus we can consider partitions where each biclique corresponds to I and J with J = complement of pi(I) within some set? But we need to partition edges: each edge (i,j) where j != pi(i) must be assigned to exactly one biclique (I,J). So each edge is uniquely assigned to a biclique. So we need to allocate each pair (i,j) to a biclique that includes I containing i and J containing j, with J disjoint from pi(I). Equivalent to a \"edge coloring\" of the complete bipartite graph minus a matching where each color class is a complete bipartite subgraph.\n\nThus the minimal number of colors needed to edge-partition K_{n,n} \\ M into bicliques is exactly 2n-2. This is reminiscent of the concept of \"biclique partition number\" of the complement of a perfect matching. I recall known results: The biclique partition number of a complete bipartite graph K_{n,n} with a perfect matching removed is exactly \\lceil \\log_2 n \\rceil + ...? Actually, I'm mixing up with boolean rank.\n\nBut we can attempt to prove lower bound of 2n-2 by counting something. For each row i, consider the edges incident to row i (n-1 edges). In any biclique partition, each biclique containing row i can cover at most one edge to column pi(i)? No, they avoid that column. So each biclique containing row i can cover edges to a set of columns J that avoids pi(i). The maximum size of J for a given biclique containing row i is at most n - |I|? Actually J cannot contain any pi(i') for i' in I. Since pi is a permutation, pi(i') are all distinct. So for a biclique with |I| = a rows, the set of columns to avoid is a distinct columns (the images under pi). So we can have at most n - a columns in J (since we must avoid those a columns). So each biclique containing a rows covers at most (n - a) edges per row. But each row has n-1 edges to cover. So if we pick a biclique with many rows, each row contributes at most n - a edges to that biclique. So we need enough bicliques to cover all edges.\n\nWe can attempt to prove lower bound by averaging. Suppose we have t bicliques partitioning the edges. Let a_k be the number of rows in biclique k (size of I_k). Then each of those rows gets (n - a_k) edges covered (the columns in J_k). So the total number of edges covered by biclique k is a_k * (n - a_k). Since the edges are disjoint across bicliques, sum over k of a_k * (n - a_k) = total edges = n(n-1). So we have sum_{k=1}^t a_k (n - a_k) = n(n-1). We want to minimize t given this constraint, with each a_k integer between 1 and n-1 (since a_k cannot be n because then J would be empty). Actually a_k can be up to n-1 because if a_k = n, then J must avoid all pi(i) for all rows, which would be the entire set of columns, leaving J empty, so no edges. So a_k <= n-1. Similarly a_k >= 1 because each biclique has at least one row.\n\nThus we need to find minimal t such that sum_{k} a_k (n - a_k) >= n(n-1). Since the sum equals exactly that, we can think of the maximum possible edges per biclique given a_k. For fixed a_k, the product a_k (n - a_k) is maximized when a_k = n/2 (if n even) giving roughly n^2/4. But we have to consider integer constraints. For n large, the maximum edges per biclique is roughly n^2/4. So the lower bound t >= (n(n-1)) / (max a_k (n - a_k)). For n=2025, max edges per biclique is floor(n^2/4). That's about 2025^2 /4 ~ (approx 4 million)/4 ~ 1 million. Actually 2025^2 = (2000+25)^2 = 4,000,000 + 2*2000*25 + 25^2 = 4,000,000 + 100,000 + 625 = 4,100,625. /4 = 1,025,156.25. So max edges per biclique ~1,025,156. So t >= n(n-1) / (n^2/4) ~ (n-1) * 4 / n ~ 4(1 - 1/n). For large n, that gives t >= 4 - small. So lower bound of about 4. That's too weak.\n\nThus we need a stronger bound.\n\nAlternative approach: The number of bicliques must be at least 2n - 2 because each row i must be \"separated\" from its missing column pi(i). The missing column pi(i) is not in any biclique containing row i. So each row i must be paired with at most n-2 columns in each biclique (since it cannot include pi(i)). But that doesn't give lower bound.\n\nBetter lower bound: Considering degrees of columns similarly. There might be known result: In a biclique partition of K_{n,n} minus a perfect matching, each row and each column must appear in at least one biclique that is \"one-sided\"? Something like each row must appear in at least one biclique where that biclique includes a column not used by any other row? Not sure.\n\nLet's think about covering all edges incident to a particular row i. Suppose we have bicliques B1, B2, ..., B_s that contain row i. The column sets J1, J2, ..., J_s are disjoint (since edges are disjoint) and their union is all columns except pi(i). Also, each Ji must be disjoint from pi(I_j) for all rows in the biclique I_j. So for each biclique containing row i, the column set Ji cannot contain pi(i') for any i' in I_j, including pi(i). So pi(i) is excluded anyway. So the column sets Ji partition the set of columns C_i = {1,...,n} \\ {pi(i)}. So we partition the n-1 columns among the bicliques that contain row i. For each biclique B_j containing row i, the column set Ji has size at most n - |I_j| (since must avoid pi(I_j)). But since i is in I_j, we have |I_j| >= 1, so size of Ji <= n - |I_j| <= n - 1. That's not helpful.\n\nBut we can try to sum over rows: The total number of (row, biclique) incidences is sum_{k} a_k (since a_k rows per biclique). Let's denote by R = sum_{k} a_k. Similarly, total number of (column, biclique) incidences is sum_{k} b_k, where b_k = |J_k|.\n\nWe have edges covered: each edge (i,j) appears in exactly one biclique. So each edge contributes to exactly one row incidence and one column incidence. So sum_{k} a_k * b_k = total edges = n(n-1). Also, sum_{k} a_k = total number of row incidences = sum_i (number of bicliques containing row i). Similarly sum_{k} b_k = total number of column incidences = sum_j (number of bicliques containing column j).\n\nSince each row i needs to cover all n-1 edges incident to it, and each biclique containing row i can cover at most b_k edges for that row (i.e., each column in J_k). So the number of bicliques containing row i is at least something like the number of column sets needed to partition the n-1 columns. But each biclique containing row i can include many columns. The minimal number of bicliques containing row i is at least the minimum number of subsets (each of size up to something) needed to partition the n-1 columns, where each subset corresponds to a biclique that also includes some other rows. So we need to consider constraints across rows.\n\nBetter approach: Consider a bipartite graph representation and recall known result: The edge partition number into complete bipartite subgraphs (biclique partition number) of K_{n,n} minus a perfect matching is exactly 2n-2. This is a known result. I can search memory: There is a theorem by Graham and Pollak? Actually Graham-Pollak theorem says that the edge set of a complete graph K_n can be partitioned into n-1 complete bipartite subgraphs. That's the Graham-Pollak theorem. Indeed, the Graham-Pollak theorem states that the edges of the complete graph K_n can be partitioned into at least n-1 complete bipartite subgraphs, and this bound is tight. That's for complete graph (not bipartite). But we have a complete bipartite graph K_{n,n} minus a perfect matching. There is a similar result: The bipartite version states that the edges of K_{n,n} can be partitioned into n complete bipartite subgraphs (stars), but we have missing edges. There's also a result: The bipartite Graham-Pollak theorem states that the edges of K_{n,n} can be partitioned into n complete bipartite subgraphs (stars). Actually K_{n,n} can be partitioned into n stars: for each i, take edges from row i to all columns. That's n bicliques (each is a star). That's a partition. So the biclique partition number of K_{n,n} is n. If we remove a perfect matching, we need more bicliques: maybe 2n-2.\n\nThe Graham-Pollak theorem for complete graph K_n says any partition of the edges into complete bipartite subgraphs (bicliques) requires at least n-1 subgraphs. For K_{n,n} that's a bipartite graph, the minimal number is n? Let's check: K_{n,n} has n^2 edges. If we try to partition into bicliques, each biclique must be of the form I x J. The sum of a_k * b_k = n^2. If we try to minimize number of bicliques t, we need a_k * b_k as large 