y edges. Hard to cover in only one more biclique.\n\nThus 3 seems impossible for n=4. But we found 4 works. So minimal for n=4 appears to be 4, which equals n? Actually n=4, minimal = 4? Let's verify if we can get 4 as lower bound: maybe minimal is n for even n? But n=3 minimal was 4, not 3. So pattern not n.\n\nWait for n=3 we found minimal 4, which is > n=3. For n=4 minimal 4 = n? Actually n=4 minimal 4 equals n. For n=2 minimal 2 = n. So pattern maybe minimal = n for even n, and minimal = n+1 for odd n? Let's test n=5 maybe minimal = 6? Let's try to find a partition for n=5 with 5 rectangles? Possibly not.\n\nWe need to find general formula.\n\nLet's test n=5 manually perhaps. Identity zeros at (i,i). We need to partition edges of K5 minus perfect matching. Could we partition into 5 bicliques? Let's try to generalize the construction we used for n=4: we used column stars (column j with all rows except row j). That gave us n bicliques (one per column). Actually we used 4 bicliques each being a column star, but we omitted column 4 star includes rows 1-3 only (excluding row4). Actually we used column stars for each column j, but each column star excludes the row j (since that edge is missing). So each column star includes all rows except the one whose missing column is that column. Thus each column star has n-1 rows, except maybe we can omit a column star? In our construction for n=4, we used all 4 columns: column 1 star covering rows 2,3,4; column 2 star covering rows 1,3,4; column 3 star covering rows 1,2,4; column 4 star covering rows 1,2,3. That's exactly 4 bicliques. So we partitioned all edges using exactly n column stars. Indeed, each edge (i,j) with i != j appears in column j star (since column star j includes all rows except row j). Since i != j, row i is included, so edge (i,j) is covered by column j star. And each edge appears only in that star because column j star is the only biclique containing column j (others may also contain column j, but they may also contain row i). But we can ensure partition if we only include column stars (each column j star covering all rows except j). Then each edge (i,j) appears exactly once (in column j star). So we have a partition of the graph into n bicliques: the column stars. That seems to be a valid partition for any n. But earlier we thought we needed 2n-2. Wait we have n bicliques! Let's verify: For n=3, column stars would be:\n\n- Column 1 star: rows 2,3 (edges (2,1),(3,1))\n- Column 2 star: rows 1,3 (edges (1,2),(3,2))\n- Column 3 star: rows 1,2 (edges (1,3),(2,3))\n\nThat's exactly 3 bicliques covering all 6 edges. But earlier we thought minimal for n=3 is 4. Let's check if the column star partition is valid: For each column j star, we include rows I_j = {i : i != j}. This set includes row j? No, we exclude row j. So each biclique includes all rows except the one whose missing column is that column. Since the missing squares are (i,i), each column star includes all rows except row j, avoiding the missing edge (j,j). The biclique includes all edges (i,j) for i != j. Indeed, those are all edges incident to column j (except the missing one). So the union of all column stars covers each edge exactly once, because each edge (i,j) belongs to column j star (and not to any other column star). So we have a perfect partition into n bicliques. So minimal number of tiles is at most n.\n\nBut earlier we thought for n=3 minimal is 4. Let's double-check with actual tiling: We need to partition the complement of the permutation matrix into rectangles. Using column stars translates to tiles that are vertical rectangles of width 1 (i.e., columns). For column j star, we have a rectangle covering rows = all rows except row j, column = j (so a vertical 1-column rectangle of height n-1). That's a tile of size (n-1) x 1. Since each tile must be a rectangle, this is fine. So for n=3, we have 3 tiles: column 1 tile covering rows 2-3, column 2 tile covering rows 1,3 (but rows 1 and 3 are not adjacent? Wait the rectangle must be contiguous rows; the tile must be a rectangle (contiguous rows and columns). The column star includes rows {2,3} for column 1: that's contiguous (rows 2-3). Good. For column 2 star includes rows {1,3} which are not contiguous (they are separated by row 2). That's not allowed as a single rectangle because there is a gap at row 2 where column 2 is missing (since (2,2) is missing). The tile would have to be a rectangle of rows 1-3, column 2, but that would include row2, column2 which is missing. So we cannot have a rectangle covering rows 1 and 3 only, leaving a hole at row2. So the column star for column 2 cannot be a single rectangle because the rows must be a contiguous interval. The set of rows {1,3} is not contiguous. So we need to split column 2 star into two rectangles: one for row 1, column 2 (1x1), and one for row 3, column 2 (1x1). That adds extra tiles. So the partition into column stars fails to respect contiguity of rows.\n\nThus the tile must be a rectangle where rows are a contiguous interval and columns are a contiguous interval. So each biclique I x J must have I and J as intervals (consecutive). That's a crucial additional constraint! Indeed, tile sides must be on grid lines, so the rectangle must be contiguous. So the biclique partition must be into \"interval bicliques\" (interval submatrices). That's more restrictive.\n\nThus the earlier construction of 2n-2 used intervals: rows intervals of length 1 (single row) and columns intervals of intervals (contiguous). For each row i, we made a rectangle covering row i and columns i+1 .. n (or columns i+1..n). That's an interval. Similarly for columns j we made rectangles covering rows j+1..n and column j (interval). So those are intervals. The column star for column j is not contiguous in rows unless we split at the missing row j. So to make intervals, we need to split column j star into two rectangles: one covering rows 1..j-1, column j; and another covering rows j+1..n, column j. That's 2(n-1) rectangles: each column yields two rectangles (except maybe first and last column? Actually column 1 yields rows 2..n, column 1 (contiguous) - it's one interval, because rows 1..0 doesn't exist. Similarly column n yields rows 1..n-1, column n, one interval. So total intervals = 2(n-1) - 2? Let's compute precisely.\n\nIf we use column intervals: For column j, we need to cover all rows except row j. So we can split into at most two intervals: rows 1..j-1 (if j > 1) and rows j+1..n (if j < n). So total number of rectangles from all columns = sum_{j=1}^n ( (j>1 ? 1 : 0) + (j<n ? 1 : 0) ) = (n-1) + (n-1) = 2n-2. Indeed, for j=1, we have only rows 2..n (one interval). For j=n, only rows 1..n-1 (one interval). For middle columns, two intervals. So total = 2n-2. This matches the earlier construction. So the 2n-2 construction is just the column-star decomposition with intervals.\n\nThus the minimal number of tiles is at most 2n-2. The question is: can we do even fewer using a mix of row intervals and column intervals, perhaps merging some intervals across columns to form larger rectangles that avoid missing squares? For n=4 we found a construction with 4 rectangles (which is less than 2n-2=6). But we need to verify if those rectangles are intervals. In our 4 rectangle construction for n=4, each rectangle was a column star (column j with rows all except j). However each column star had rows that are not contiguous except maybe column 1 star has rows 2,3,4 which are contiguous (rows 2-4). That's fine. Column 2 star has rows 1,3,4 => not contiguous (1 and 3,4). But we used column 2 star with rows {1,3,4}. That is not contiguous. Wait we listed C3: column 2 with rows {1,3,4}. That set is not contiguous because missing row 2. So we cannot have a rectangle that includes rows 1,3,4 but not row 2. So that construction is invalid for tiling with rectangles. Indeed, our rectangle must be contiguous rows. So the column-star decomposition into intervals (splitting into two intervals) yields 6 rectangles for n=4, not 4. Our earlier 4 biclique partition used non-interval sets, so it's invalid.\n\nThus the constraint that each rectangle must be axis-aligned contiguous intervals is crucial. So the lower bound may be 2n-2. But perhaps we can do better than 2n-2 by using rectangles that are not just columns or rows, but larger blocks that avoid zeros by being placed in \"staircase\" shape? For example, we can have a rectangle that covers rows i..j and columns p..q, as long as there is no zero within that submatrix. The zeros are at positions (k, pi(k)). So a rectangle must avoid any such positions. So for a given interval of rows I = [a,b] and columns J = [c,d], we must have for all k in I, pi(k) not in J. That is, the missing columns for rows in I must all lie outside J. So we need J to be a subset of columns that does not intersect pi(I). So if we choose I = some set of rows, we must avoid the columns that are images of those rows under pi. So the allowed columns are the complement of pi(I). For a given I, the maximum allowed J is the set of columns not in pi(I). In principle we could choose J to be all columns except pi(I), but J must be an interval. So we need an interval that avoids the set pi(I). Since pi(I) could be scattered, it's not possible to have a large interval that avoids them unless pi(I) is away from the interval. So the shape of pi matters: if pi is monotone (i.e., identity), then pi(I) = I (the same indices). So to avoid pi(I), we must choose J that does not intersect I, i.e., J must be either entirely left of I or entirely right of I. So for identity, any rectangle must be wholly above the diagonal, below the diagonal, or cross but avoid diagonal entries. Actually if I = rows [a,b] and J = columns [c,d] with c > b (i.e., J to the right of I), then pi(I) = I is not in J, so allowed. Similarly if d < a (J left of I). So rectangles can be placed either entirely above the main diagonal (i.e., rows less than columns)