rts across rows produce a shape that is a Young diagram (maybe not monotone decreasing lengths). Similarly right parts produce another shape. We can argue that each of these shapes requires at least n-1 rectangles to partition, regardless of permutation. Let's examine.\n\nConsider the left side of each missing square: For each row i, define L_i = {columns j < pi(i)}; the left part of row i. Let L_i be possibly empty. The collection of these left parts across rows forms a shape that is a Young diagram rotated? Actually if we sort rows by the missing column index pi(i), we can see that the sizes of L_i are monotone decreasing? Not necessarily: pi(i) could be any permutation, so the left side lengths can be arbitrary. But we can consider the shape formed by all squares left of missing squares: the set S = {(i,j) : j < pi(i)}. This shape is exactly the set of squares that are to the left of the missing square in each row. Similarly the right side shape T = {(i,j) : j > pi(i)}. These two shapes partition the complement (since any square must be either left or right of the missing square in its row). So any rectangle that covers squares from both left and right sides of a row is impossible because it would cross the missing square. So each rectangle must be wholly within S or wholly within T. So we can treat the problem as tiling S and T separately. Since S and T are each shapes with at most n-1 rows (maybe some rows have size 0). But they are \"Ferrers diagrams\"? Let's examine S.\n\nDefine for each row i, the left part length L_i = pi(i)-1 (since columns 1..pi(i)-1). So L_i values are a permutation of numbers 0..n-1 (since pi(i) runs over 1..n). Indeed, the multiset {L_i} = {0,1,2,...,n-1} because pi(i) runs over all values. So there is exactly one row with left part length 0 (the row where pi(i)=1), one row with left part length 1 (pi(i)=2), etc. So the left side shape S consists of rows of lengths 0,1,...,n-1, each length occurring exactly once. However the rows are not necessarily sorted by length; they appear in arbitrary order. So S is a Ferrers diagram after permuting rows. But the shape S is a \"permuted\" Young diagram: rows of lengths 0..n-1 in some order.\n\nNow we ask: what is the minimal number of axis-aligned rectangles needed to partition S? Since the rows can be in any order, perhaps we can partition S more efficiently than n-1? We need to consider that rectangles must be contiguous rows and columns. Because rows are not sorted by length, you might be able to combine rows of varying lengths into rectangles that cover columns up to the smallest length among them, etc.\n\nBut note that any rectangle in S must be comprised of rows I and columns J such that for each row i in I, J \u2286 [1, pi(i)-1]. Since pi(i)-1 = L_i is the max column index allowed for that row (since columns are left of missing square). So J must be a subset of the intersection of intervals [1, L_i] across rows i in I. Intersection of intervals [1, L_i] = [1, min_{i\u2208I} L_i]. So any rectangle in S can be defined by a set of rows I and a column interval J = [1, c] where c \u2264 min_{i\u2208I} L_i. So rectangles are anchored at column 1 (they always start at column 1) and extend to some column c \u2264 min(L_i). Because they must be leftmost contiguous block from column 1 onward. Since J must be contiguous and start at column 1 maybe not necessarily; could start at column a>1 if all rows have L_i >= a, but then we would be missing the leftmost columns for those rows (which might be covered by other rectangles). However due to the shape, any rectangle can be placed somewhere within the left region, not necessarily anchored at column 1. For a given row i with left length L_i, the allowed columns are 1..L_i. So a rectangle can be placed in columns a..b where a >= 1 and b \u2264 L_i for all rows i in I. So the intersection of allowed intervals for rows is [1, min L_i]. So any rectangle's column interval must be a subinterval of [1, min L_i]. So it can be placed anywhere inside that, but can't exceed that min.\n\nThus the shape S is like a set of rows of varying lengths, each row being a left-justified interval from column 1 to L_i. So S is a \"Ferrers board\" but rows may not be monotonic: the lengths are in arbitrary order. However the shape's columns also have varying heights: column j is present in those rows i where L_i \u2265 j, i.e., rows where pi(i) > j. Since pi is a permutation, column j has exactly n-j rows (since pi(i) > j means pi(i) \u2208 {j+1,...,n}, which are n-j values). So column j has exactly n-j rows occupied (i.e., squares in S). So the column heights are monotonic decreasing: column 1 has n-1 rows (since all rows except the one with pi(i)=1 have left part length >=1). Actually column 1 is present in all rows where pi(i) > 1, i.e., all rows except the one where pi(i)=1. So column 1 has n-1 squares. Column 2 has n-2 squares (all rows except those where pi(i) <=2). In general, column j has n-j squares. So the shape S is a \"standard Young diagram\" shape of partition (n-1,n-2,...,1) after possibly permuting rows? But the shape is exactly the same as the upper triangular shape when rows are sorted by L_i decreasing. However the rows are not sorted, but the shape as a set of squares is exactly the same as the upper triangular region: the set of squares (i,j) such that j < pi(i). Since pi is a permutation, the condition j < pi(i) is equivalent to (i,j) with j < pi(i). But if we reorder rows according to pi(i), we get the shape of a Young diagram. However the actual arrangement of squares in the grid is not contiguous rows; the rows are in original order, but the shape S is still a set of squares that is a \"Ferrers diagram\" but possibly \"skewed\". However for tiling with rectangles, the order of rows matters because rectangles must be contiguous in original row ordering. So the shape may be more complicated.\n\nNevertheless, we can consider the \"profile\" of S: For each row i, we have left segment of length L_i. The lengths L_i are a permutation of 0..n-1. So the shape is essentially a \"staircase\" but rows are permuted. This shape is known as a \"permutation diagram\" for the inverse permutation? Actually the set of points (i, pi(i)) is the permutation matrix; the squares to the left of the points form a Young diagram shape after sorting rows by pi(i). But after permuting rows, the shape is a \"permutation diagram\" which is a 0-1 matrix with ones left of the permutation points and zeros elsewhere. This is known as a \"permutation matrix's lower-left region\". The shape is a \"matrix of shape of a permutation\". It is known that the minimal number of rectangles needed to partition such a shape is exactly n-1, regardless of row ordering. Let's verify.\n\nThe shape S is a \"board\" that is the complement of the permutation pattern above the main diagonal after applying a permutation of rows. The shape is known to be a \"Ferrers board\" after sorting rows appropriately; but before sorting, it's a \"permutation board\". However, the minimal number of rectangles needed to partition it might be n-1 because each rectangle can cover at most one \"corner\" of the shape? Let's examine.\n\nConsider the shape S: it's a monotone shape: If you look at any column j, the rows that have squares in that column are exactly those rows i where L_i >= j, i.e., pi(i) > j. Since pi(i) > j is a set of rows that is a permutation of some numbers. This set is not necessarily contiguous in row order: it could be any subset of rows of size n-j. So column j squares may be scattered across rows, not forming a contiguous vertical strip. So shape S is not necessarily a Young diagram; it's more like a \"rook diagram\".\n\nThus tiling S with rectangles may be more complex. However we can still tile S by splitting each column into at most two intervals (similar to earlier column splitting). Because for column j, the missing square is at row i where pi(i) = j. So column j has a missing square at row i. So the present squares in column j are rows above i (1..i-1) and rows below i (i+1..n). So they are at most two intervals. So we can partition S into at most 2n-2 rectangles (the same 2n-2 construction). But we need a lower bound.\n\nThe earlier lower bound for identity case used distinct row lengths argument. But for arbitrary permutation, the shape S may be more \"jagged\". Could it be partitioned into fewer rectangles than n-1? Possibly yes, because rows of varying lengths may allow merging. Let's test with a small n=3 permutation (identity). We found minimal = 4 overall (so each side needed 2 rectangles). That's n-1 = 2 for each side. Could there be a permutation where left side shape can be tiled with only 1 rectangle? For n=3, left side lengths are L_i = pi(i)-1. The multiset is {0,1,2}. Could we tile left side shape with only 1 rectangle? That would require a rectangle covering all left side squares. The left side squares are:\n\n- Row with L_i=0: no squares.\n- Row with L_i=1: column 1.\n- Row with L_i=2: columns 1-2.\n\nIf the rows with L_i=1 and L_i=2 are contiguous (i.e., appear consecutively in original ordering), then we could have a rectangle covering rows of those two rows and columns 1-2? But the row with L_i=1 only has column 1; column 2 is missing in that row (since L_i=1). So rectangle rows i1,i2 columns 1-2 would include a missing square at (row with L_i=1, col2). So not allowed. So you can't cover left side with a single rectangle. So need at least 2 rectangles. So for n=3 left side minimal = 2.\n\nFor n=4, left side lengths are {0,1,2,3}. Could we tile left side shape with 2 rectangles? Possibly. Let's try to construct a permutation where left side shape can be tiled with 2 rectangles. For that, we need to partition the left side squares into 2 rectangles. Since there are 6 squares left side (sum of lengths = 0+1+2+3 = 6). Each rectangle must be contiguous rows and columns. Could we have rectangle covering rows with L_i >=2 and columns 1-2? Let's see. Suppose we have rows with L_i >=2: those rows have lengths 2 or 3. Let's say we have rows A (L=2) and B (L=3). Row A has columns 1,2; Row B has 1,2,3. If we choose rectangle rows A,B and columns 1-2, that's possible (since both rows have columns 1-2 filled). That rectangle would cover squares: (A,1),(A,2),(B,1),(B,2). Good. That covers 4 squares. The remaining squares are: row with L=1 (col1), row with L=3 (col3). Actually row B had col3 also left side square. Row with L=1 has col1. So we need to cover those squares with maybe two 1x1 rectangles: (row L=1, col1) and (row B, col3). That's total 3 rectangles. So left side would require at least 3 rectangles (maybe more). So not 2.\n\nBut maybe we can use rectangles differently: a rectangle covering rows with L_i >=1 (i.e., rows with lengths 1,2,3) and columns 1-1 (just column1). That covers column 1 squares in rows with lengths >=1. That's rows with L_i >=1, which are three rows. That's one rectangle of size 3x1 covering column1. Then remaining squares: column2 in rows with L_i >=2 (two rows), column3 in row with L_i=3 (one row). Those could be covered by a rectangle rows with L_i >=2, columns 2-2 (vertical rectangle) covering column2 squares (size 2x1). And a rectangle covering (row with L_i=3, col3) (1x1). That's total 3 rectangles again. So left side minimal maybe 3 for n=4. Indeed n-1 = 3, matches.\n\nThus for left side shape, minimal number of rectangles needed seems to be at least n-1 for any permutation. Similarly for right side shape. So total minimal rectangles needed at least 2(n-1) = 2n-2.\n\nThus the answer is 2n-2 = 4048.\n\nBut we need to be thorough: Provide a rigorous argument for lower bound: Show that any tiling must use at least n-1 tiles in the left region (or right region). So we need to prove that for any permutation pi, the left region S = {(i,j) | j < pi(i)} requires at least n-1 rectangles. This is akin to a known combinatorial fact: The left region is a \"Ferrers board\" after sorting columns, but in the original ordering, you can't reduce the number of rectangles below n-1 due to the monotonic decreasing column heights.\n\nOne approach: Consider the columns of S. Column j has exactly n-j squares. Since they are not contiguous in rows, you cannot cover all squares in column j with a single rectangle (unless you also include some rows that are not contiguous). However you can split column j into intervals of consecutive rows, each of which can be covered by a vertical rectangle. The number of intervals needed for column j is at least the number of \"gaps\" in the set of rows where pi(i) > j. The set of rows where pi(i) > j is the set of rows i such that pi(i) > j. Since pi is a permutation, the rows where pi(i) > j are exactly the rows i for which pi(i) belongs to {j+1,...,n}. The positions of these rows in the ordering can be arbitrary, but we can think about the number of \"runs\" of such rows. For each column j, the rows where pi(i) > j form some subset of rows. The number of runs (maximal consecutive sequences) in this subset is at least 1 (if nonempty). Actually could be many. But we need a lower bound on total number of rectangles across all columns.\n\nAlternatively, use an argument based on \"increasing sequence\" of columns: Since column heights are decreasing, any rectangle covering some set of columns must have its rows covering only rows that are present in all those columns. The intersection of row sets for columns j and j+1 is the set of rows where pi(i) > j+1 (since they need to be present in both columns). So the intersection size decreases as j increases.\n\nWe can perhaps use a counting argument: Let the tiling be a set of rectangles. For each rectangle, define its rightmost column r and leftmost column l. Since rectangles cannot cross the missing squares, a rectangle's column interval J must be contained entirely within a gap between consecutive values of pi(i) for rows in I. Since the missing squares correspond to a set of columns each appearing exactly once across rows, the gaps are intervals of columns that contain no pi(i) values for rows in I. The number of such gaps is at most something.\n\nBetter: Use the concept of \"staircase decomposition\" of permutations: The complement of the permutation matrix can be decomposed into monotone decreasing and increasing sequences of rectangles. It is known that any permutation matrix can be partitioned into 2n-2 monotone rectangles, and this is optimal. This is essentially the result we need.\n\nThus answer: 4048.\n\nBut we need to present reasoning clearly.\n\nWe can adopt the following structure:\n\n1. Represent uncovered squares as a permutation matrix: there exists a permutation \u03c0 of {1,\u2026,2025} such that uncovered squares are (i,\u03c0(i)). So each row i has missing column \u03c0(i).\n\n2. The remaining squares are partitioned into two sets: Left side L = { (i,j) : j < \u03c0(i) } and Right side R = { (i,j) : j > \u03c0(i) }. Because a tile cannot cross a missing square in its row, any tile must be wholly contained in L or R. So we treat L and R separately.\n\n3. Observe that |L| = sum_{i=1}^{2025} (\u03c0(i)-1) = (0+1+...+2024) = (2025*2024)/2 = ... Similarly |R| = same.\n\n4. Show that any tiling of L requires at least n-1 tiles. Same for R. So total at least 2n-2.\n\nProof for L: Consider the columns 1,\u2026,n-1. For each column j, the set of squares in L in that column is exactly the set of rows i with \u03c0(i) > j. Since \u03c0 is a permutation, there are exactly n-j such rows. Among those rows, consider the minimal and maximal row indices. Because rows are arranged in natural order, the set of rows may be split into at most something. But we can use a combinatorial argument: The columns have decreasing numbers of squares: column 1 has n-1 squares, column 2 has n-2, ..., column n-1 has 1 square. For any rectangle, the number of squares it contributes to column j is either 0 or equal to the number of rows it spans (its height). Since column heights differ, we can bound the number of rectangles needed by something like a pigeonhole principle: Each rectangle can \"cover\" at most one square in the column with smallest height? No.\n\nAlternatively, use the following known theorem: For any Ferrers board (a Young diagram shape), the minimum number of rectangles needed to partition it is equal to the number of distinct row lengths (or column heights). In L, the row lengths are a permutation of {0,...,n-1}, thus there are n distinct lengths (including 0). But we exclude rows of length 0 (they contain no squares). So there are n-1 distinct positive lengths. Thus any partition of L into rectangles must use at least n-1 rectangles. This is because each rectangle can have a set of rows with a common minimal length among them, and each rectangle reduces the number of distinct lengths by at most 1. Formal proof: Suppose we have a partition of L into rectangles. Let \u2113 be the smallest positive row length among rows not yet covered. The rectangle covering a row of length \u2113 can only cover columns up to \u2113, so after covering that row, the next smallest positive length among uncovered rows must be at least \u2113+1, etc. This yields at least n-1 rectangles needed. Or we can use a more rigorous argument by induction: In any rectangle covering some rows, the rectangle's rightmost column is limited by the smallest row length among those rows. Remove the rectangle; the set of row lengths of the remaining uncovered squares will still contain all lengths from that smallest length+1 up to n-1. Hence each rectangle eliminates at most one distinct row length. Since there are n-1 distinct positive lengths, we need at least n-1 rectangles.\n\nLet's formalize: Let the rows be ordered arbitrarily (original order). For each row i, define \u2113_i = \u03c0(i) - 1 (left length). Let L_i denote the set of squares in row i belonging to L. In any rectangle R covering rows I and columns J, the rightmost column of R is at most min_{i\u2208I} \u2113_i. So after using rectangle R, the rows I will have all squares up to that column covered, but any row i\u2208I may still have uncovered squares to the right of that column (if \u2113_i > min \u2113). However those squares will be in columns > min \u2113, which are not reachable by any rectangle that also includes a row with a smaller \u2113 (since they would intersect missing squares). So the rows with the smallest \u2113 among all uncovered rows must be covered by a rectangle that uses the column interval ending at that \u2113. This rectangle can cover at most one such smallest \u2113 because any rectangle that includes a row with \u2113_min also cannot include any row with \u2113' < \u2113_min (none) but can include rows with \u2113' > \u2113_min, but then the rectangle's rightmost column is limited to \u2113_min, so it cannot cover squares in columns > \u2113_min for the rows with larger \u2113. Those squares will require additional rectangles. So each distinct \u2113 requires at least one rectangle. Since \u2113 values range from 1 to n-1 (distinct), we need at least n-1 rectangles. This is a known argument for Ferrers board.\n\nThus any tiling of L needs at least n-1 tiles. Similarly for R (where right lengths = n - \u03c0(i)). Since the set of right lengths also is a permutation of {0,...,n-1}, we need at least n-1 tiles for R. So total at least 2n-2.\n\nConstruction achieving this lower bound: Use the simple column-splitting method described earlier: For each column j, draw two vertical tiles: one covering the squares above the missing square in that column, and one covering squares below it (if any). This yields exactly 2n-2 tiles. (The topmost column where missing square is in row 1 yields only the lower part; similarly for column where missing square is in row n yields only the upper part.)\n\nThus the minimum number of tiles is exactly 2n-2. For n=2025, this equals 4048.\n\nThus answer: 4048.\n\nNow we should also verify that the problem statement says \"minimum number of tiles needed\". It does not ask for the arrangement. So answer is 4048.\n\nWe should present reasoning: lower bound and construction.\n\nWe'll also mention that k = sqrt(2025) = 45, might be irrelevant but maybe they want to hint about splitting the board into k x k blocks? But the solution is independent of k.\n\nLet's produce final answer.\n\nBut we need to be thorough and provide a clear explanation for the lower bound.\n\nWe could also present a combinatorial argument using an invariant: For each i from 1 to n-1, consider the set of squares that lie either left of the missing squares and have column index i (i.e., column i). There are exactly n-i squares in this set. In any tile, the number of squares in this set that belong to the tile is either 0 or equal to the height of the tile (the number of rows it spans). Since the heights of tiles are limited by the fact that rows containing missing squares at column i cannot be included in any tile covering column i (since that missing square would be included). The rows that can be in a tile covering column i are either all rows above the missing row in column i or all rows below. Thus each tile can cover at most one contiguous segment of column i. There are exactly n-i rows that must be covered in column i, and they form two contiguous segments (above and below). So we need at least (n-i) segments? Wait each tile can cover at most one contiguous segment per column. So the total number of tiles must be at least the total number of segments across all columns? This leads to lower bound 2n-2.\n\nBut we need a crisp argument.\n\nAnother approach: Define \"breakpoints\" in rows: For each row i, the missing square splits the row into two parts: left part (columns < \u03c0(i)) and right part (columns > \u03c0(i)). Any tile cannot include squares from both sides of that missing square. So each tile is either wholly left or wholly right. Define L-tiles and R-tiles. Each L-tile covers a set of squares in the left parts of some rows. For a given column j, the left parts of rows that include column j are exactly those rows i with \u03c0(i) > j. In these rows, column j appears in the left part. These rows can be split into possibly several blocks separated by rows where \u03c0(i) \u2264 j (i.e., missing square is at or left of column j). But since each column has exactly one missing square, the rows where \u03c0(i) = j are exactly one row; rows where \u03c0(i) < j are those where missing column is left of column j; rows where \u03c0(i) > j are those where missing column is right of column j. So the rows where column j appears in left parts are exactly the rows where missing column is to the right (i.e., \u03c0(i) > j). Among those rows, the missing row at column j is the row where \u03c0(i) = j, which is not included. So the rows where column j appears in left parts are all rows except those where \u03c0(i) \u2264 j. Since the rows where \u03c0(i) \u2264 j may be scattered, the set of rows where column j is present may be in multiple intervals. But we can show that the number of intervals is at most 2 (above and below the row where \u03c0(i) = j). Indeed, columns j is present in all rows except the row where missing square is at column j and rows where missing column is left of j? Wait left of j means \u03c0(i) < j; those rows have missing square left of column j, so column j is to the right of missing square, thus belongs to right part, not left part. So column j appears in left part only for rows where \u03c0(i) > j. Those rows are the set of rows whose missing column is to the right of j. Since \u03c0 is a permutation, the row where \u03c0(i) = j is exactly one row; all other rows have missing column either less than j or greater than j. So the rows where column j appears in left part are exactly those rows where \u03c0(i) > j, which is a set of size n-j. This set is split into two intervals: rows before the row where \u03c0(i) = j (if any), and rows after that row. Because rows are ordered 1..n, the row where \u03c0(i) = j occurs somewhere, dividing the rows into those above and those below. Among rows above, some may have \u03c0(i) > j, some may have \u03c0(i) < j. But the set of rows above the row where \u03c0(i) = j that have \u03c0(i) > j could be non-consecutive because there could be rows above with \u03c0(i) < j interspersed. However, can that happen? Let's examine. Suppose we have rows i1 < i2 < i3, with \u03c0(i1) > j, \u03c0(i2) < j, \u03c0(i3) > j. Then column j appears in left part for i1 and i3 but not i2. So the set of rows where column j appears in left part is not contiguous. So it may be more than two intervals. Wait earlier we thought column j missing square splits column into at most two intervals (above and below). But we need to consider left vs right parts. Actually column j squares belong to left part for rows where \u03c0(i) > j and to right part for rows where \u03c0(i) < j. The missing square at row where \u03c0(i) = j is excluded. So column j squares are partitioned into left part and right part. The left part squares are those rows where \u03c0(i) > j. Those rows may not be contiguous. So column j may have many left-part intervals. However, we can consider tiling left part region L as vertical rectangles: each rectangle for column j can be a vertical segment of contiguous rows where column j is present in left part. But these segments may be many, not just one or two. So the column-splitting approach (splitting each column into at most 2 intervals) works for the entire region (both left and right) because each column j has exactly one missing square at row r (where \u03c0(r)=j). The squares above r (rows 1..r-1) in column j are all present (either in left or right part?), Actually rows above r: for row i < r, does column j belong to left or right part? It depends on whether \u03c0(i) > j or \u03c0(i) < j. But since i < r, we can't guarantee. So the squares above r may be present (if column j is not missing for those rows). But they might be on left or right side relative to missing square of that row. However, regardless, they are present squares. So column j's present squares are rows 1..r-1 and rows r+1..n (all rows except r). So they form exactly two intervals. Indeed, column j is missing only at row r; all other rows have a square at (i,j). So the set of present squares in column j is rows [1,r-1] \u222a [r+1,n], which is exactly two intervals (or one if r=1 or r=n). So column j can be covered by at most two vertical rectangles, each covering a contiguous segment of rows. So column splitting yields at most 2n-2 rectangles total. This is a valid tiling, regardless of left/right classification.\n\nThus we have an upper bound.\n\nNow the lower bound: We need to argue that any tiling must have at least 2n-2 rectangles. We can use the left/right argument: Since each row has missing square splitting it, any rectangle cannot cross that missing column. So each rectangle is either left or right of the missing column for all rows it covers. So we can color rectangles as left or right. There are at most n-1 left rectangles and n-1 right rectangles needed. But why at least n-1 each? We can argue using column counts: For each column j, there is exactly one missing square in that column at row r = \u03c0^{-1}(j). Consider the set of rectangles that cover squares in column j. Since any rectangle covering column j cannot include row r (because that square is missing). Thus any rectangle covering column j must lie entirely either above row r or below row r. So each rectangle covering column j contributes a contiguous vertical segment either entirely above or entirely below r. Since we need to cover all rows except r, we need at least one rectangle to cover the segment above r (if any) and at least one rectangle to cover the segment below r (if any). So for each column j (except possibly the topmost and bottommost columns where the missing row is at the extreme), we need at least two rectangles (one above, one below). However, a given rectangle might cover multiple columns. So counting column by column may overcount. But we can use a more clever counting.\n\nConsider the columns where the missing row is not at the top or bottom: there are n-2 such columns (all columns except those where missing row is at row 1 or n). For each such column, the missing square splits the column into a top segment (rows 1..r-1) and a bottom segment (rows r+1..n). In any tiling, each of these segments must be covered by at least one rectangle (since rectangles cannot cross missing square). However, a rectangle could cover the top segment of several columns simultaneously, as long as its row interval is a subset of rows 1..r-1 for each column, i.e., the rectangle's bottom row must be less than r for each column it covers. Since r varies across columns, the rectangle can only simultaneously cover top segments of columns with missing rows all below some row bound. Similarly for bottom segments.\n\nThus we can consider a set of columns sorted by the row index of their missing square. Let\u2019s define array a_j = \u03c0^{-1}(j), the row index for missing at column j. This is a permutation of 1..n. Sort columns by a_j. The sequence a_j is a permutation. Consider the columns in increasing order of a_j. The top segments of these columns form a \"staircase\" shape similar to the left region. The minimal number of rectangles needed to cover all top segments is at least n-1 (by the earlier argument). Similarly for bottom segments.\n\nThus any tiling must use at least n-1 rectangles for the top parts and at least n-1 for the bottom parts, giving total at least 2n-2.\n\nAlternatively, we can argue by focusing on \"critical columns\" where the missing row is not at the extreme, and using induction: Choose the column j with smallest missing row index among columns not at 1 or n, etc.\n\nBut we can present a simpler argument: Consider the set of squares that are above the missing square in each column (the \"upper region\"). This region is exactly the set U = {(i,j): i < \u03c0^{-1}(j)}. Similarly the lower region D = {(i,j): i > \u03c0^{-1}(j)}. The two regions partition the complement. Observe that each region is isomorphic to the left region of some permutation (by swapping rows and columns). Because the mapping i \u2194 \u03c0^{-1}(j) is a permutation. So each region has the same structure as the left part region, which we already argued needs at least n-1 rectangles. Hence total at least 2n-2.\n\nThus the minimal number for any permutation is exactly 2n-2.\n\nThus answer is 4048.\n\nNow we should produce a final answer.\n\nBut also note that the problem statement mentions \"k = \u221a2025 = 45\". Perhaps they intend to consider a decomposition into k x k blocks? Maybe they want to use a property of squares of size k^2? Let's think: They might want to partition the grid into k x k blocks (45x45 blocks). Then each block will contain one missing square perhaps? Actually each row and each column has exactly one missing square, so overall there are 2025 missing squares. If we partition into 45x45 blocks (i.e., 45 rows of blocks, each block of size 45x45), we have 45^2 = 2025 blocks. Since there are exactly 2025 missing squares, we could aim to place exactly one missing square per block. Then each block is a 45x45 subgrid with exactly one missing square. Could we tile each block independently with minimal number of tiles? For a 45x45 block with one missing square, the minimal number of tiles needed is 2*45 - 2 = 88? Actually for an n x n block with one missing square per row/column within the block? Wait each block would have size 45x45 and contain exactly one missing square (since there are 2025 missing squares total). However, the condition that each row and column of the whole grid has exactly one missing square does not guarantee that each block will have exactly one missing; i