set all > max row index, etc.\n\nSpecifically, for any rectangle with row interval [a,b] and column interval [c,d], we need to ensure that for all i in [a,b], j in [c,d], i \u2260 j. Since i and j can vary, we need [a,b] and [c,d] to be disjoint intervals. That is, either b < c or d < a. So the rectangle must be placed wholly either above the diagonal (b < c) or below (d < a). So the problem reduces to partitioning the region above diagonal (i<j) into rectangles where each rectangle's row interval and column interval are disjoint and column interval lies strictly to the right of row interval. Similarly for below diagonal.\n\nNow, we can try to partition the entire region above diagonal with k*(k-1)/2 cells. For n=9, that's 36 cells above diagonal, plus 36 below, total 72 cells. We need to partition into rectangles.\n\nOne naive way: For each row i, we could have a rectangle covering row i columns i+1 to n (i.e., the cells to the right of the diagonal). That's a rectangle of size 1 \u00d7 (n-i) for each i. So we would have n-1 rectangles (since row 9 has none). That's 8 rectangles for above diagonal. Similarly for below diagonal, we could have rectangles for each column, but that would double count? Actually we could also cover below diagonal by column rectangles: for each column j, rectangle covering rows j+1 to n (i.e., below diagonal). That's also 8 rectangles. But these rectangles are disjoint? Let's check: The above-diagonal rectangle for row i: cells (i, j) for j>i. The below-diagonal rectangles for column j: cells (i,j) for i>j. These are disjoint and cover all cells off diagonal. So total rectangles = (n-1)+(n-1) = 2n-2 = 16 for n=9. That's far bigger than 7.\n\nWe can do better by merging rectangles across multiple rows and columns. For example, consider rectangle covering rows 1..2, columns 3..9 (i.e., rows 1-2, columns >2). This rectangle is disjoint from diagonal because any i in {1,2} and j\u22653 => i\u2260j. So we can combine rows 1 and 2 into a single