ch tile, consider the rows it covers. Since each row has exactly one hole, each tile can include at most one interval per row (left or right). So each tile covers cells either to the left or right of the hole for each row it includes. For each tile, define its \"type\": the set of rows for which it is left of the hole (i.e., its columns are less than the hole column) and the set where it's right of the hole (columns greater). Actually as earlier, the tile's column interval must avoid all hole columns of its rows. So the column interval lies in a gap of the sorted set of hole columns for those rows. Thus the set of rows covered by a tile cannot be all rows, because then the column interval would have to avoid all hole columns -> impossible because there are n holes covering all columns. So any tile must miss at least one column (the column of a hole for some row). So each tile excludes at least one column.\n\nIf we have m tiles, the total number of columns excluded by all tiles (counted with multiplicity) is at least m (since each tile excludes at least one column). But each column is excluded by at most ??? Actually a column can be excluded by many tiles (if those tiles don't include that column). But we can bound something else.\n\nBetter: Consider the bipartite graph where vertices are rows and columns. Each tile corresponds to a complete bipartite subgraph between its rows and columns. Since each tile's column set C avoids \u03c3(R), the set of hole columns for its rows. So for each tile, there is a matching between rows and columns (the holes) that are not covered. Maybe we can use Hall's theorem to bound number of tiles.\n\nAlternatively, consider the adjacency matrix of uncovered cells (holes) as a permutation matrix P. The complement matrix Q = J - P is the matrix of cells to be tiled. The rectangle partition number of Q is known to be n+1 for n\u22653? Let's see if there is known result: The bipartite dimension of the complement of a perfect matching in K_{n,n} is n+1. Actually the bipartite dimension (minimum number of complete bipartite subgraphs needed to partition the edges of K_{n,n} - M) might be n+1. Let's test with small n: n=2: K_{2,2} - M has edges (1,2),(2,1). Partition into 2 bicliques (edges). So bipartite dimension = 2 = n? Not n+1. For n=3: we need at least 4 bicliques? Let's test: edges are all except (1,1),(2,2),(3,3). We can partition into 4 bicliques: {1}\u00d7{2,3}, {2}\u00d7{1,3}, {3}\u00d7{1,2} (that's 3) plus maybe one more to cover something? Actually these three bicliques already cover all edges: {1}\u00d7{2,3} covers (1,2),(1,3); {2}\u00d7{1,3} covers (2,1),(2,3); {3}\u00d7{1,2} covers (3,1),(3,2). That's all 6 edges. So partition uses 3 bicliques = n. So dimension = n.",
