          "reasoning": "We need to solve a combinatorial tiling problem: We have an n\u00d7n grid of unit squares (n=2025). We can place axis-aligned rectangular tiles (size any integer dimensions) covering unit squares; each tile must be placed on grid lines and cannot overlap (i.e., each unit square covered at most once). Additionally, each row must have exactly one uncovered unit square (i.e., one \"hole\" per row), and each column must have exactly one uncovered unit square. So the uncovered squares form a set of n unit squares such that exactly one per row and one per column; thus they form a permutation matrix: there is exactly one uncovered square in each row and column, i.e., a set of n squares that is a transversal (a set of n distinct rows and columns). Thus uncovered squares correspond to a permutation \u03c3: for each row i, column \u03c3(i) is uncovered. So we need to tile the rest of the squares (n\u00b2-n squares) with rectangles, possibly of varying sizes, covering all other squares exactly once. We need to minimize the number of rectangles (tiles). Equivalent to covering all squares except those in a permutation matrix with rectangles.\n\nGoal: find minimal number of rectangles needed to tile an n\u00d7n board with one missing square per row and column. The missing squares are at distinct rows and columns, essentially a rook placement.\n\nWe need to find minimal number of rectangles to tile the rest of the board. For n=4 (k=2), answer is 5 tiles. We need to propose a formula for general n (2025, but also test small n). Let's think.\n\nFirst, note that each rectangle can be considered as covering a submatrix of contiguous rows and columns. The uncovered squares break the board into rectangles (connected components) if we consider the missing squares as obstacles; but we can also produce rectangles that bypass the holes? Actually rectangles cannot contain uncovered squares because no unit square can be covered more than once and uncovered squares must