l > max row index, etc.\n\nSpecifically, for any rectangle with row interval [a,b] and column interval [c,d], we need to ensure that for all i in [a,b], j in [c,d], i \u2260 j. Since i and j can vary, we need [a,b] and [c,d] to be disjoint intervals. That is, either b < c or d < a. So the rectangle must be placed wholly either above the diagonal (b < c) or below (d < a). So the problem reduces to partitioning the region above diagonal (i<j) into rectangles where each rectangle's row interval and column interval are disjoint and column interval lies strictly to the right of row interval. Similarly for below diagonal.\n\nNow, we can try to partition the entire region above diagonal with k*(k-1)/2 cells. For n=9, that's 36 cells above diagonal, plus 36 below, total 72 cells. We need to partition into rectangles.\n\nOne naive way: For each row i, we could have a rectangle covering row i columns i+1 to n (i.e., the cells to the right of the diagonal). That's a rectangle of size 1 \u00d7 (n-i) for each i. So we would have n-1 rectangles (since row 9 has none). That's 8 rectangles for above diagonal. Similarly for below diagonal, we could have rectangles for each column, but that would double count? Actually we could also cover below diagonal by column rectangles: for each column j, rectangle covering rows j+1 to n (i.e., below diagonal). That's also 8 rectangles. But these rectangles are disjoint? Let's check: The above-diagonal rectangle for row i: cells (i, j) for j>i. The below-diagonal rectangles for column j: cells (i,j) for i>j. These are disjoint and cover all cells off diagonal. So total rectangles = (n-1)+(n-1) = 2n-2 = 16 for n=9. That's far bigger than 7.\n\nWe can do better by merging rectangles across multiple rows and columns. For example, consider rectangle covering rows 1..2, columns 3..9 (i.e., rows 1-2, columns >2). This rectangle is disjoint from diagonal because any i in {1,2} and j\u22653 => i\u2260j. So we can combine rows 1 and 2 into a single rectangle. Similarly, we can combine rows 3..4 with columns 5..9, etc. So we can partition the upper triangle into a few rectangles arranged like a staircase. For n=9, we could partition by grouping rows in intervals and columns accordingly.\n\nPotentially, we can partition the whole board (with diagonal holes) into 2k-1 rectangles: k rectangles covering the upper triangular region, and k-1 covering the lower triangular region, or vice versa. Let's examine.\n\nIf we choose row intervals [1, k], [k+1, 2k], [2k+1, 3k], ...? Since n = k^2, we can think of grouping rows into k groups of size k each. Then for each group of rows, we can choose column intervals that are to the right of those rows. For group g (rows (g-1)k+1 to gk), the rightmost column index for each row is at least row+1. The columns to the right of the entire group start from (g-1)k+1? Actually need to ensure columns interval does not intersect any row index within the group. The minimal column index that is always > any row in the group is (gk)+1 (i.e., one beyond the max row index in the group). So we can take rectangle covering rows of group g, columns from (gk+1) to n. So this rectangle covers all cells above diagonal for those rows beyond the group. That leaves some cells above diagonal within the group but to the left of that column interval (i.e., columns within same group but > row). For those, we can further partition recursively within the group.\n\nThus we can recursively partition the board into a hierarchical structure. This suggests a recursion that yields something like 2k - 1 rectangles maybe.\n\nAlternatively, maybe minimal number is n + sqrt(n) - 1? For n=4, n + sqrt(n) - 1 = 4+2-1=5, matches. For n=9, that would be 9+3-1=11, not 7. So not.\n\nMaybe minimal number is 2 sqrt(n) + 1? For n=4: 2*2+1=5 matches. For n=9: 2*3+1=7. Could be plausible. For n=1 (trivial): sqrt(1)=1 => 2*1+1=3, but we have n=1 board with one uncovered square per row and column: there is exactly one row and column, the