tiling for n=k^2 with k+1 rectangles.\n\nWe can aim to tile as follows:\n\n- Use k rectangles covering the \"upper off-diagonal\" region: For each block index a=1..k, define rectangle R_a covering rows of block a (rows (a-1)k+1 .. a*k) and columns of blocks > a (i.e., columns (a*k+1) .. n). This region is contiguous: rows are a block of size k, columns are a contiguous interval from a*k+1 to n (i.e., columns belonging to blocks a+1,...,k). Since each row in block a has its hole column in block a (if we set holes within same block), the columns in blocks > a contain no holes for those rows. So R_a is safe. Similarly, we can define k rectangles covering the \"lower off-diagonal\" region: for each block index a=1..k, define rectangle L_a covering rows of blocks > a (i.e., rows a*k+1 .. n) and columns of block a (columns (a-1)k+1 .. a*k). Those are also safe. But these 2k rectangles double count some region? Actually the upper and lower off-diagonal regions partition the board into two halves with the diagonal blocks left out. Specifically, the upper off-diagonal region is rows in block a, columns in blocks > a. The lower off-diagonal region is rows in blocks > a, columns in block a. Together they cover all off-diagonal cells exactly once (as each off-diagonal cell is either above diagonal (row block < column block) or below diagonal (row block > column block)). So we have total of k(k-1) cells in each half? Actually number of rectangles: For each a, we have one rectangle for upper region (if we treat all rows in block a combined) and one rectangle for lower region (if we treat all rows > a combined?). Wait we need to be careful: For each a from 1 to k-1 maybe we have rectangles. Let's think.\n\nDefine for each a (1 \u2264 a \u2264 k-1), rectangle U_a = rows in block a (size k) and columns in blocks a+1 through k (size (k-a)*k). That's a rectangle of size k \u00d7 ((k-a)k). Similarly for each a (2 \u2264 a \u2264 k), rectangle L_a = rows in blocks a+1 through k (size (k