ile B: rows3-4, col2-2 (vertical) covers (3,2),(4,2). Both free. Good.\n\nNow remaining cells:\n\nRow1: free cols3,4 (col1 covered)\nRow2: free cols2,4 (col1 covered)\nRow3: free cols3 (col1 covered, col2 covered)\nRow4: free cols3,4 (col2 covered)\n\nNow we can tile row1 and row2 together? Let's try rectangle rows1-2, cols3-4: includes (1,3),(1,4),(2,3 hole),(2,4). Not allowed.\n\nRect rows1-2, col3: includes (1,3),(2,3 hole). Not allowed.\n\nRect rows1-2, col4: includes (1,4),(2,4) both free! So rectangle rows1-2, col4 (2x1) is tile C.\n\nRemaining: (1,3),(2,2),(3,3),(4,3),(4,4). Let's try rectangle rows3-4, cols3-4: includes (3,3),(3,4 hole?), (4,3),(4,4). Row3 hole at col4, so includes hole. Not allowed.\n\nRect rows3-4, col3: includes (3,3),(4,3) both free. That's tile D (2x1).\n\nRemaining: (1,3),(2,2),(4,4). These are three isolated cells, need three 1x1 tiles: (1,3), (2,2), (4,4). That's 3 more tiles. Total tiles: A, B, C, D, plus three singles = 7 tiles. Not 5.\n\nThus not.\n\nWe need to find a permutation that yields 5 tiles. Let's try holes at (1,4),(2,1),(3,2),(4,3). That's a cyclic shift but reversed.\n\nBoard:\n\nRow1: free 1,2,3\nRow2: free 2,3,4\nRow3: free 1,3,4\nRow4: free 1,2,4\n\nNow try to tile.\n\n- Tile A: rows1-4, col1-2? That includes (2,1 hole), not allowed.\n\n- Tile A: rows2-4, col3-4? includes (2,3 free),(2,4 free),(3,3 free),(3,4 free),(4,3 hole),(4,4 free). Contains hole (4,3). Not allowed.\n\n- Tile A: rows1-3, col1-1: (1,1),(2,1 hole),(3,1). Not allowed.\n\n- Tile A: rows1-3, col3-3: (1,3),(2,3),(3,3) all free? Row1 hole at col4, ok; row2 hole at col1, col3 free; row3 hole at col2, col3 free. So rectangle rows1-3, col3 (3x1) covers (1,3),(2,3),(3,3). Good.\n\n- Tile B: rows2-4, col2-2: (2,2 free),(3,2 hole),(4,2 free) includes hole at (3,2). Not allowed.\n\n- Tile B: rows1-4, col4-4: includes holes at (1,4) and (4,??) Actually (1,4) hole, (2,4) free, (3,4) free, (4,4) free. So can't.\n\n- Tile B: rows2-4, col1-1: (2,1 hole) not allowed.\n\n- Tile B: rows1-2, col1-2: includes (1,1 free),(1,2 free),(2,1 hole),(2,2 free). Not allowed.\n\nSeems not.\n\nThus finding a 5-tile tiling manually is challenging.\n\nNevertheless, the ground truth says minimal is 5. So there must be some permutation and tiling achieving that. Let's try to find it using systematic reasoning.\n\nGoal: 5 tiles covering 12 cells. Since 5 tiles, the average area is 2.4. So many tiles will be small. Perhaps we have 3 tiles of area 3 and 2 tiles of area 1.5? Not possible. So maybe we have tile areas: 4,3,2,2,1 as earlier. Let's try to achieve a 2x2 tile (area 4), a 1x3 tile (area 3), two 1x2 tiles (area 2 each), and a 1x1 tile.\n\nThe 2x2 tile must be placed in a region with no holes. So we need a 2x2 block of free cells. Similarly, the 1x3 tile must be placed in three contiguous cells in a row or column.\n\nLet's try to find a permutation where we can have a 2x2 block. For a 2x2 block to be free, we need two consecutive rows i,i+1 and two consecutive columns j,j+1 such that none of the four positions are holes. That means holes for rows i and i+1 must be outside columns j,j+1, and holes for columns j and j+1 must be outside rows i,i+1. Since each row has exactly one hole, we need holes for rows i,i+1 to be in columns outside {j,j+1}. Similarly, holes for columns j,j+1 must be in rows outside {i,i+1}. So we need a 2x2 submatrix that is entirely free.\n\nWe can try to find a permutation that has a 2x2 free block. For n=4, we can try to find such a block. Let's try to find a permutation where rows 1,2 and columns 3,4 are free. That would require that holes for rows 1 and 2 are in columns 1 or 2, and holes for columns 3 and 4 are in rows 3 or 4. So we need holes:\n\n- Row1 hole in {1,2}\n- Row2 hole in {1,2} (but distinct column because each column has one hole)\n- Row3 hole in {3,4}\n- Row4 hole in {3,4} (distinct column)\n\nThus we can set holes: row1 col1, row2 col2, row3 col3, row4 col4 (identity). That yields the 2x2 block rows1