\nWe can try to find large rectangles.\n\nObserve that column 1 has holes at row2 only. So column1 free cells at rows1,3,4. These rows are not contiguous (row2 missing). So any rectangle covering column1 must be either rows1-1 (just row1), rows3-4 (contiguous), or rows1-4 (includes row2 hole). So we could have rectangle rows3-4, col1 (2x1). Similarly column2 has hole at row4; free cells rows1-3 (contiguous), so rectangle rows1-3, col2 (3x1). Column3 hole at row1; free rows2-4 (contiguous), rectangle rows2-4, col3 (3x1). Column4 hole at row3; free rows1-2,4 (not contiguous). So we could have rectangle rows1-2, col4 (2x1) and rectangle row4, col4 (1x1). This yields many rectangles: col1: (3-4,1) (2x1), col2: (1-3,2) (3x1), col3: (2-4,3) (3x1), col4: (1-2,4) (2x1) + (4,4) (1x1). That's 5 rectangles plus maybe some leftover? Let's list covered cells:\n\n- (3,1),(4,1) covered.\n- (1,2),(2,2),(3,2) covered.\n- (2,3),(3,3),(4,3) covered.\n- (1,4),(2,4) covered.\n- (4,4) covered.\n\nNow check all free cells: We need to ensure we covered all free cells. Let's list all free cells:\n\nRow1: col1, col2, col4 (col3 hole). We covered (1,2) and (1,4) but (1,1) not covered. So need rectangle covering (1,1). Could add (1,1) as 1x1 tile, increasing count to 6.\n\nThus not good.\n\nThus far we haven't found 5 tiles for n=4. Let's try systematic search: maybe the minimal tiling uses rectangles of sizes: 3+3+3+2+1? Actually sum must be 12. Could be 3+3+2+2+2 =12 (5 rectangles). Let's try to achieve three 2x? rectangles.\n\nWe need to find a permutation and tiling achieving 5.\n\nGiven exhaustive search confirms minimal 5, there must be some configuration. Let's try to guess it.\n\nConsider holes at (1,2),(2,4),(3,1),(4,3) as earlier. Let's try to tile differently.\n\nFree cells:\n\nRow1: (1,1),(1,3),(1,4)\nRow2: (2,1),(2,2),(2,3)\nRow3: (3,2),(3,3),(3,4)\nRow4: (4,1),(4,2),(4,4)\n\nWe can try to tile as:\n\n- Tile A: rows1-2, cols1-1 (vertical: (1,1),(2,1)) -> 2x1.\n- Tile B: rows3-4, cols2-2 (vertical: (3,2),(4,2)) -> 2x1.\n- Tile C: rows1-2, cols3-4 (2x2): includes (1,3),(1,4),(2,3),(2,4 hole). So not allowed.\n\n- Tile C: rows3-4, cols3-4 (2x2): includes (3,3),(3,4),(4,3 hole),(4,4) includes hole, not allowed.\n\n- Tile C: rows1-4, cols4-4 (vertical: (1,4),(2,4 hole),(3,4),(4,4)) includes hole at (2,4), not allowed.\n\n- Tile C: rows1-4, cols1-1 includes hole at (3,1). Not allowed.\n\n- Let's try to cover (1,3),(2,2),(3,3),(4,1) maybe as a rectangle? Not contiguous.\n\nAlternative approach: maybe we can tile with one 3x2 rectangle covering rows1-3, columns3-4? That includes (1,3),(1,4),(2,3),(2,4 hole),(3,3),(3,4). Contains hole (2,4). Not allowed.\n\nTry rectangle rows2-4, cols1-2: includes (2,1),(2,2),(3,1 hole),(3,2),(4,1),(4,2). Contains hole (3,1). Not allowed.\n\nTry rectangle rows1-3, cols1-2: includes (1,1),(1,2 hole),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1 hole),(3,2). Contains holes (1,2),(3,1). Not allowed.\n\nThus not.\n\nMaybe the minimal tiling uses a rectangle that is 3x3 covering most cells, with holes at corners of that rectangle? Let's think: Suppose we have a 3x3 block of free cells but with holes at (1,1) and (3,3) maybe? But we only have 4 holes total. Could arrange holes such that they are all at corners of a 3x3 block? Let's try holes at (1,1),(2,4),(3,4?), not.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can tile with a 2x3 rectangle covering 6 cells, another 2x3 covering 6 cells, total 2 rectangles = 12 cells, but we have holes that would be inside? Could we place holes such that they are outside these two rectangles? For two 2x3 rectangles to be disjoint and cover 12 cells, they must cover the entire board except the holes. So each rectangle must be placed in a region that does not contain holes. Since each row has a hole, each row must be split into left and right intervals. A 2x3 rectangle covers 2 rows; for each of those rows, the rectangle's columns must avoid the hole column in that row. So the rectangle's columns must be either left of both holes or right of both holes. So each rectangle can cover at most the left (or right) intervals for two rows.\n\nThus to cover a row's left interval (size maybe up to k-1) we need at least one rectangle per interval group.\n\nThus maybe minimal number equals 2k+1 indeed.\n\nLet's attempt to compute f(n) for n = k^2 = 9 using a pattern that yields 7 tiles.\n\nWe need to design holes such that we can tile with 7 rectangles.\n\nWe can try to use horizontal strips: group rows into k=3 groups of size 3 (which is the whole board). So we have only one group. Not helpful.\n\nBetter: Use k=3: we can try to tile with 2k+1 = 7 rectangles. Let's try to design a tiling with 7 rectangles for n=9.\n\nOne approach: Use 3 rectangles covering columns 1-3 for rows 4-9? Not.\n\nLet's try to systematically think: We need to cover left region (size 36) and right region (size 36). Perhaps we can cover left region with 3 rectangles and right region with 4 rectangles (or vice versa). That totals 7.\n\nThus we need to find a permutation such that left region can be tiled with 3 rectangles. How can left region be tiled with 3 rectangles for n=9? The left region has column heights (8,7,6,5,4,3,2,1,0). To cover with 3 rectangles, each rectangle must be a submatrix of rows and columns, covering some cells. The rectangles must partition the left region.\n\nGiven column1 has 8 cells, column2 has 7 cells, etc. With 3 rectangles, each rectangle can cover multiple columns, but must avoid holes for rows it includes. We need to assign rows to rectangles such that the columns for those rows' left intervals are contiguous and can be covered with one rectangle.\n\nOne way: Use rectangle covering rows 2-9 (rows 2..9) and columns 1..1 (col1). That covers column1 cells for rows 2-9 (8 cells). Column1 has 8 cells, exactly those rows (since row1 left length is 0). So rectangle1 = rows2-9, column1 (size 8x1). Good.\n\nNow left region remaining cells: column2 cells (rows3-9) (7 cells). Column3 cells (rows4-9) (6 cells), etc.\n\nWe can similarly use rectangle covering rows3-9, columns2-2 (size 7x1). That's rectangle2.\n\nSimilarly rectangle covering rows4-9, columns3-3 (size 6x1) rectangle3. But then we have used 8 rectangles (one per column). So not good.\n\nBut perhaps we can combine columns 1 and 2 into a rectangle covering rows3-9, columns1-2? Let's check: For rows3-9, column1 and column2 are both left of the hole for those rows if the holes for those rows are at columns >=3. Indeed, if the permutation \u03c3(i) for rows i >=3 is >= i+something? Wait we need to ensure that for rows 3-9, the holes are to the right of column2 (i.e., \u03c3(i) > 2). Since there is exactly one hole per column, columns 1 and 2 are used as holes in some rows. Those rows cannot be included in rectangle covering columns1-2. So we need to choose rows that avoid holes in columns1 and 2. That means we need to exclude the rows where holes are at column1 and column2. So we can choose rectangle covering rows that are not those rows.\n\nThus to combine columns, we need to group rows that have holes all to the right of the column interval.\n\nThus we need to choose a permutation such that holes in columns 1..c are all located in a set of rows that we can exclude from the rectangle covering those columns. Then the remaining rows have holes to the right, allowing rectangle covering those columns across those rows.\n\nThus we can try to arrange holes such that holes in columns 1..m are located in the top m rows, and holes in columns m+1..n are located in bottom n-m rows. Then we can have a rectangle covering bottom rows and left columns (or top rows and right columns). This is similar to a block diagonal arrangement.\n\nSpecifically, suppose we choose a permutation \u03c3 such that \u03c3(i) = i for i = 1..k (hole at column i in row i). Then for rows i > k, holes are at columns i (maybe). Actually identity. Then we have holes on diagonal. The left region is lower triangle.\n\nBut we can rearrange rows and columns (by renaming) to get a different shape.\n\nConsider a permutation that splits the board into two blocks: first t rows have holes in first t columns (forming a t\u00d7t block), and remaining n - t rows have holes in remaining n - t columns. This is essentially a block diagonal permutation (like identity but possibly with block size t). Then left region consists of lower triangle within each block and some region between blocks. This might allow tiling left region with fewer rectangles.\n\nLet's try for n=9, choose t=3. So holes: rows 1,2,3 have holes at columns 1,2,3 (some permutation within that block). Rows 4,5,6 have holes at columns 4,5,6. Rows7,8,9 have holes at columns7,8,9. That's a block diagonal permutation (three blocks of size 3). In each block, the left region within the block is a lower triangle of size 3 (3 cells). The left region across blocks consists of blocks below the diagonal blocks: rows 4-9, columns 1-3 (full block of size 6x3 = 18 cells), rows 7-9, columns 4-6 (3x3=9 cells). Actually we need to compute left region: cells (i,j) with j < \u03c3(i). Since \u03c3(i) is within same block as i, for rows in block 2 (rows 4-6), \u03c3(i) is between 4-6, so j < \u03c3(i) includes columns j in earlier blocks (1-3) and also columns within block 2 that are less than \u03c3(i). So left region includes full blocks of earlier columns for those rows.\n\nThus left region includes large rectangles: for rows 4-9, columns 1-3 (since all rows in blocks 2 and 3 have holes in columns >=4, thus columns 1-3 are left of holes). That's a rectangle of size (6 rows) x (3 columns) = 18 cells. Similarly, rows 7-9, columns 4-6 (3 rows x 3 columns) = 9 cells. Also within each block, we have lower triangle cells.\n\nThus left region can be partitioned into rectangles: main rectangle (rows 4-9, cols 1-3) as one tile; rectangle (rows 7-9, cols 4-6) as another tile; plus lower triangle cells within each block: each block of size 3 has lower triangle of 3 cells, which can be covered 